const TOPICS = [
  // ─────────────────── NOTIFICATION SERVICE ─────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m5", "lld_m5_t7", 7, "Design a Notification Service", "design-notification-service",
    ["case-study", "strategy", "observer", "reliability"],
    "An order ships. The customer should get a push notification, an email with a tracking link, and — only if the delivery is late — an SMS. They have muted marketing email, they are asleep in Sydney, and the shipping service just retried the same event three times because your acknowledgement timed out. How many notifications should they receive, and how does your design guarantee that number? Design the Notification Service.",
    "Two axes carry this design and they are orthogonal. WHAT the message looks like on each medium is a CHANNEL STRATEGY plus a rendered TEMPLATE — email, SMS, push and in-app share one send() contract and nothing else. WHETHER a message is delivered at all, exactly once, is a reliability protocol: an IDEMPOTENCY KEY derived from the event (not from a random UUID) makes a replayed send a no-op, retries use exponential backoff with jitter into a dead-letter queue, and user preferences plus quiet hours are resolved BEFORE the channel is ever touched. Every serious follow-up in this interview is 'what happens when the send fails halfway'.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: any service in the company (orders, payments, social graph, security) can publish an EVENT — order_shipped, password_reset, comment_reply — and the notification service turns that event into zero or more messages delivered on zero or more CHANNELS: email, SMS, mobile push, in-app inbox, and increasingly WhatsApp or webhooks. Each notification TYPE has a TEMPLATE per channel and per locale, rendered with variables from the event payload; the same event produces a 40-character SMS and a full HTML email from one input. Users control what they receive: per-type, per-channel opt-in/opt-out, QUIET HOURS in their own timezone, digest preferences ('bundle these into one daily email'), and a global unsubscribe that is legally binding for marketing but must NOT suppress transactional messages like a password reset. The service must expose delivery status (queued, sent, delivered, bounced, failed) and support scheduled and recurring sends. Non-functional constraints are what shape the classes: upstream event buses are AT-LEAST-ONCE, so the same event WILL arrive twice and the service must not double-notify; downstream providers (SES, Twilio, FCM) fail transiently, rate-limit you, and occasionally accept a request and deliver nothing, so retries are mandatory and must be safe; fan-out can be large (a comment on a popular thread notifies 5,000 watchers), so the API must be non-blocking and the work queued; and a slow SMS provider must never stall email delivery, which means per-channel isolation. Clarify scope with the interviewer: single service, providers behind adapters, the queue abstracted behind an interface, no multi-region concerns."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "NOTIFICATIONEVENT is the immutable input: eventType, recipientId (or a recipient resolver for fan-out), a payload map, an eventId from the producer, and an occurredAt timestamp. NOTIFICATIONREQUEST is what the service derives per recipient per channel: the resolved channel, the template key, the merged variables, a priority, and — the load-bearing field — an IDEMPOTENCYKEY. CHANNEL is the Strategy interface: send(RenderedMessage) returning a Receipt (providerMessageId, status) or throwing a typed error that distinguishes RETRYABLE (timeout, 5xx, throttled) from PERMANENT (invalid address, unsubscribed, hard bounce); EmailChannel, SmsChannel, PushChannel and InAppChannel implement it, each wrapping a provider adapter. TEMPLATE is (templateKey, channel, locale) -> a compiled template with a declared variable set; TEMPLATEENGINE renders it and is responsible for channel-correct escaping — HTML-escape for email, no escaping but a hard length budget for SMS. PREFERENCERESOLVER answers one question — 'may I send type T to user U on channel C right now?' — folding opt-outs, quiet hours in the user's timezone, per-channel throttles, and the transactional override into a single Decision with a reason. DELIVERYTRACKER owns the mutable delivery record keyed by idempotencyKey: state (PENDING, SENT, DELIVERED, FAILED, SUPPRESSED), attempt count, next attempt time, provider receipts. RETRYPOLICY computes the next delay; DEADLETTERQUEUE holds what exhausted it. NOTIFICATIONSERVICE is the facade: resolve recipients, resolve preferences, render, enqueue, and let workers drive channels."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "Three patterns, each with a defence. STRATEGY for channels: send() is a genuinely varying algorithm with a stable signature, so the dispatcher holds Map<ChannelType, Channel> and never branches on channel type — adding WhatsApp is one class plus one registry entry, and the compiler tells you what a new channel must implement. The alternative, a switch on an enum inside a sendNotification() method, forces every channel's provider quirks into one class and is exactly what the interviewer adds a fifth channel to expose. OBSERVER for fan-out: publishers emit events without knowing who listens; the notification service SUBSCRIBES to the event bus, and within the service a notification type maps to a recipient resolver (the author of the post, everyone watching the thread, all members of a workspace). This keeps the ordering service ignorant of email — the coupling direction that lets notification logic change weekly without redeploying commerce. TEMPLATE METHOD (or a small pipeline of steps) for the send flow itself: resolve -> filter by preference -> render -> dispatch -> track, with each step overridable per channel; SMS overrides render with truncation, push overrides dispatch with token-per-device fan-out. Two structural decisions matter as much as the patterns. First, the public API is ASYNCHRONOUS: notify() validates, persists a delivery record, enqueues, and returns — never blocking a checkout thread on Twilio. Second, the QUEUE IS PARTITIONED PER CHANNEL (and often per priority), so a throttled SMS provider backs up its own queue while email drains normally; one shared queue with mixed work is the head-of-line blocking bug you will be asked about. Preference resolution deliberately happens BEFORE enqueue AND is re-checked at send, because a user can unsubscribe while a message sits in the queue."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "interface Channel {\n  ChannelType type();\n  Receipt send(RenderedMessage msg) throws ChannelException;   // typed: retryable vs permanent\n}\n\nfinal class RenderedMessage {\n  final String to, subject, body, idempotencyKey;\n  RenderedMessage(String to, String subject, String body, String key) {\n    this.to = to; this.subject = subject; this.body = body; this.idempotencyKey = key;\n  }\n}\n\nclass NotificationService {\n  private final Map<ChannelType, Channel> channels;      // Strategy registry\n  private final PreferenceResolver prefs;\n  private final TemplateEngine templates;\n  private final DeliveryTracker tracker;\n  private final Queue<NotificationRequest> queue;\n  private final RetryPolicy retry;\n\n  /** Idempotency key is DERIVED from the event, never random: a replay maps to the same key. */\n  static String keyFor(NotificationEvent e, String userId, ChannelType ch) {\n    return sha256(e.eventType() + \"|\" + e.eventId() + \"|\" + userId + \"|\" + ch);\n  }\n\n  public void notify(NotificationEvent e) {\n    for (String userId : resolveRecipients(e)) {                 // Observer fan-out\n      for (ChannelType ch : channelsFor(e.eventType())) {\n        Decision d = prefs.decide(userId, e.eventType(), ch, e.occurredAt());\n        if (d.suppressed()) { tracker.suppress(keyFor(e, userId, ch), d.reason()); continue; }\n        NotificationRequest req = new NotificationRequest(\n            e, userId, ch, keyFor(e, userId, ch), d.deferUntil());  // quiet hours -> deferUntil\n        if (tracker.claim(req.idempotencyKey())) queue.offer(req);   // atomic insert-if-absent\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  /** Worker loop: one per channel partition. */\n  public void process(NotificationRequest req) {\n    DeliveryRecord rec = tracker.get(req.idempotencyKey());\n    if (rec.state() == State.SENT || rec.state() == State.DELIVERED) return;   // replay = no-op\n    if (!prefs.decide(req.userId(), req.eventType(), req.channel(), now()).allowed()) {\n      tracker.suppress(req.idempotencyKey(), \"opted out after enqueue\"); return;\n    }\n    RenderedMessage msg = templates.render(req.templateKey(), req.channel(),\n                                           req.locale(), req.vars(), req.idempotencyKey());\n    try {\n      Receipt r = channels.get(req.channel()).send(msg);\n      tracker.markSent(req.idempotencyKey(), r.providerMessageId());\n    } catch (ChannelException ex) {\n      if (!ex.retryable() || rec.attempts() + 1 >= retry.maxAttempts()) {\n        tracker.markFailed(req.idempotencyKey(), ex.getMessage());\n        deadLetter.add(req);\n      } else {\n        long delayMs = retry.nextDelayMs(rec.attempts());        // 2^n * base + jitter\n        tracker.scheduleRetry(req.idempotencyKey(), now() + delayMs);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nclass RetryPolicy {\n  private final long baseMs = 1_000; private final int max = 6;\n  int maxAttempts() { return max; }\n  long nextDelayMs(int attempt) {\n    long exp = baseMs * (1L << Math.min(attempt, 10));           // 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s...\n    long jitter = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextLong(exp / 2); // decorrelate the herd\n    return Math.min(exp / 2 + jitter, 15 * 60_000L);             // cap at 15 minutes\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: channels as Strategy and templates as rendered data",
        "Start with what varies. A notification has a stable SHAPE — recipient, content, tracking — and a wildly varying DELIVERY MECHANICS: email needs a subject, MIME parts, HTML plus a plaintext alternative, and an unsubscribe header; SMS has a 160-character segment budget, no markup, and per-country sender rules; push needs a device-token list (one user, five devices), a payload under 4KB, and a per-platform envelope; in-app is just a database row plus a websocket nudge. Strategy is the right cut because the VARIATION IS PER-MEDIUM AND TOTAL: no shared code survives between building a MIME message and building an APNs payload, so trying to unify them inside one method produces a parameter list that grows a field per channel. Behind Channel sits a second, smaller adapter seam: SmsChannel talks to a provider adapter, so swapping Twilio for a cheaper regional carrier changes an adapter, not the channel semantics, and provider failover becomes a composite Channel that tries adapters in order. TEMPLATE RENDERING is where juniors under-design. The naive version concatenates strings at the call site, which fatally couples the producing service to copy and locale. The correct model treats a template as versioned DATA looked up by (templateKey, channel, locale) with a declared variable set, precompiled at load so rendering is a fast tree walk rather than a regex pass. Four rules earn their place: fall back through locale (fr-CA -> fr -> en) so a missing translation degrades instead of throwing; treat a MISSING variable as a hard error at publish time via the declared variable set, because 'Hi ${firstName}' rendering literally to a customer is the classic production embarrassment; escape per channel — HTML-escape email bodies to stop content injection, and count GSM-7 segments for SMS so a stray emoji does not silently triple the cost; and keep templates deployable independently of code so marketing edits copy without a release. Finally, render LATE, in the worker, not at publish: content, locale, and even opt-in state can change between enqueue and send, and re-rendering costs microseconds while sending a stale message costs trust."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: delivery reliability — idempotency keys, retries, and the double-notify",
        "This is the mechanism the interview is actually about. The pipeline is at-least-once end to end: the upstream bus redelivers when your ack times out, your own worker can crash between provider call and state write, and the provider itself may time out after having queued the message. Naive retry logic — catch, sleep, call send() again — produces the failure everyone has lived through: three identical 'your payment failed' SMS at 2am. The fix is an IDEMPOTENCY KEY that is DERIVED, not generated: key = hash(eventType, producerEventId, userId, channel). Because it is a pure function of the event, a replayed event computes the SAME key, and the DeliveryTracker's claim(key) is an atomic insert-if-absent (a unique index on the key, or SETNX in Redis) whose failure means 'someone already owns this send' — the replay exits at line one. Note carefully what that requires of producers: the producer must supply a STABLE eventId. If it stamps a fresh UUID on each retry you have no dedupe basis at all, and saying that out loud — that idempotency is a contract with the producer, not a trick inside your service — is senior signal. The second half is the RETRY LOOP. Classify errors first: 4xx invalid-recipient, hard bounce, or unsubscribed are PERMANENT — retrying burns quota and can get your sender domain blocked; timeouts, 429s, and 5xx are RETRYABLE. Retryable failures reschedule with EXPONENTIAL BACKOFF (1s, 2s, 4s, ... capped at minutes) plus JITTER, because a provider outage fails ten thousand messages simultaneously and un-jittered backoff makes them all retry in the same millisecond, re-DDoSing the provider the instant it recovers. After maxAttempts the record goes to a DEAD-LETTER QUEUE with the last error, so failures are inspectable rather than silently gone. The residual honesty: retrying a request that the provider actually received but failed to acknowledge WILL duplicate unless the provider itself dedupes — so pass your idempotency key through to providers that support one (Twilio, Stripe, SES message deduplication), and state plainly that without provider-side support the guarantee degrades to at-least-once with a small duplicate window. Exactly-once delivery does not exist; exactly-once EFFECT via a derived key does."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: preferences, quiet hours, and fan-out that does not stampede",
        "PREFERENCE RESOLUTION looks like a lookup and is really a small policy engine, and interviewers push on it because getting it wrong is a legal problem, not just a bug. Resolve in a fixed precedence: (1) is this TRANSACTIONAL? password resets, security alerts, and receipts ignore marketing opt-outs and quiet hours entirely — classify every notification type as transactional or promotional at definition time, because a global 'unsubscribe from everything' that also kills password resets is a support catastrophe; (2) global unsubscribe; (3) per-type per-channel preference; (4) QUIET HOURS. Quiet hours are the subtle one: they are defined in the USER'S timezone, so the comparison must convert the send instant into their local time — and DST means storing an IANA zone id ('Australia/Sydney'), never a fixed UTC offset, or twice a year every user in a DST region gets notified an hour early. A quiet-hours hit is not a drop: it returns deferUntil = the local window's end, and the message is scheduled rather than suppressed — unless it is transactional, which sends immediately. Return a DECISION OBJECT carrying allowed/suppressed/deferred plus the reason so support can answer 'why did I not get the email' from data instead of by re-reading code. FAN-OUT: a comment on a busy thread notifies thousands. Do not fan out synchronously inside notify() — resolve recipients in batches and enqueue per recipient, so the API returns in milliseconds and one failing recipient cannot fail the batch. The Observer pattern gives the decoupling, but two protections come with scale: DEDUPLICATION ACROSS EVENTS (a user watching a thread AND mentioned in the comment should get one notification, not two — collapse by (userId, entityId) within a window), and DIGESTING (per-user rate caps that convert the 40th notification of the hour into a bundled summary; model it as a per-user token bucket plus a digest accumulator, and note that digests change the idempotency key's grain from event to window). Both are policy on top of the same pipeline, which is the design's proof that the separation held."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SYNCHRONOUS vs QUEUED SEND: calling the provider inline is simplest and gives the caller a real status, but it couples checkout latency to Twilio's p99 and makes retries the caller's problem; queueing decouples and enables retry/backoff/priority, at the cost of eventual delivery status and a queue to operate. Every real system queues; say why, and note the exception — an OTP that the user is staring at is sometimes sent inline with a tight timeout because a 30-second queue delay is a failed login. PER-CHANNEL QUEUES vs ONE QUEUE: one queue is less infrastructure but suffers head-of-line blocking when one provider throttles; per-channel (and per-priority) partitions isolate failure at the cost of more consumers to size. PUSH-ALL vs PULL INBOX: in-app notifications can be written eagerly to each recipient (fast reads, expensive fan-out for huge audiences) or computed on read from a feed (cheap writes, slower reads) — the fan-out-on-write vs fan-out-on-read trade, and for broadcast-to-a-million the answer flips to read-time. TRACK EVERY MESSAGE vs SAMPLE: a delivery record per message per channel is the audit trail support needs and the storage bill nobody budgets; TTL the terminal records and keep aggregates. TEMPLATES IN DB vs IN CODE: DB templates ship without deploys and let marketing self-serve, but lose compile-time variable checking — mitigate with a declared variable schema and a preview/lint step in the admin tool. Extension points to name: SCHEDULED and RECURRING sends (a due-time index the workers poll, reusing the same idempotency machinery with a period-stamped key); PROVIDER FAILOVER as a composite Channel with circuit breakers per provider; WEBHOOKS as just another Channel — which is the design's real test, since adding a channel that needs signing and customer-controlled endpoints should still be one class; and A/B testing of copy as a template-selection strategy, not an if-statement in the sender."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'The event bus redelivers the same order_shipped three times — how many emails?' — one; the idempotency key is a hash of the producer's stable eventId plus user and channel, and claim() is an atomic insert-if-absent, so replays lose the race and exit. 'What if the provider timed out but actually sent?' — you cannot distinguish it locally; forward your idempotency key to the provider if it supports one, otherwise accept a narrow duplicate window and say so — exactly-once delivery is not achievable, exactly-once effect via a derived key is the honest target. 'Add WhatsApp next sprint.' — one Channel implementation plus a registry entry plus templates for the new channel; the dispatcher, preferences, retry and tracking are untouched, which is the whole point of the Strategy cut. 'The user muted notifications after the message was queued.' — re-check preferences in the worker immediately before send, not only at enqueue; the queue can hold a message for minutes. 'It is 3am for the recipient.' — quiet hours resolve in the user's IANA timezone and return deferUntil rather than dropping; transactional types bypass entirely. 'One provider is down and 50k messages are failing.' — exponential backoff with jitter prevents the synchronized retry stampede, a circuit breaker sheds calls while it is open, and the DLQ captures what exhausts retries so nothing is lost silently. 'A popular post notifies 10,000 watchers.' — batch recipient resolution, enqueue per recipient so failures are isolated, collapse duplicate reasons per user, and apply per-user rate caps that roll into a digest. 'How does this become a distributed system?' — the classes barely change: the queue becomes Kafka or SQS, DeliveryTracker becomes a keyed store with a unique index, workers scale per channel partition, and the idempotency key is what makes horizontal scaling safe — the LLD-to-HLD seam is exactly that key."),
    ],
    "Notification service is the most common 'design a service' LLD prompt after parking lot, and it separates candidates fast because it has two independent difficulty axes. The first is extensibility — interviewers add a channel and a template variant to see whether you built a Strategy registry or a switch statement. The second, and the one that decides seniority, is reliability: at-least-once event buses plus flaky providers mean you must volunteer idempotency keys derived from the event, retry with exponential backoff and jitter, retryable-vs-permanent error classification, and a dead-letter queue — unprompted. Preference and quiet-hour resolution adds a timezone/DST trap and a legal distinction between transactional and promotional messages that production engineers know and beginners never mention.",
    [
      G("random_idempotency_key", "Generating a fresh UUID per send attempt (or having no key at all) and calling it idempotency, so a replayed event produces a second notification.", "Derive the key deterministically from the event — hash(eventType, producerEventId, userId, channel) — and make claim(key) an atomic insert-if-absent on a unique index; state that a stable producer eventId is a contract, not an assumption.", "UUID.randomUUID() assigned inside the send path; dedupe implemented as 'select then insert'; duplicate notifications reproduced by replaying one event."),
      G("retry_without_backoff_or_classification", "Retrying every failure immediately and identically — including permanent ones like invalid address or hard bounce — with no jitter, cap, or dead-letter path.", "Classify errors as retryable (timeout, 429, 5xx) vs permanent (invalid recipient, unsubscribed, hard bounce); reschedule retryables with exponential backoff plus jitter and a cap, then dead-letter after maxAttempts so nothing fails silently.", "catch (Exception e) { send(msg); } in a loop; fixed sleep between attempts; no attempt counter on the delivery record; provider re-flooded the moment it recovers."),
      G("channel_switch_statement", "One sendNotification() method with a switch on channel type, and preferences/quiet hours checked only at enqueue time (or ignored) with quiet hours stored as a fixed UTC offset.", "Make each channel a Strategy in a registry; resolve preferences through one PreferenceResolver returning allowed/suppressed/deferred with a reason, re-checked in the worker, using IANA timezone ids so DST is handled.", "if (channel == EMAIL) ... else if (channel == SMS); quiet hours compared against a stored integer offset; transactional messages suppressed by a marketing unsubscribe."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.notif, "Notification service class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── LOGGING FRAMEWORK ────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m5", "lld_m5_t8", 8, "Design a Logging Framework", "design-logging-framework",
    ["case-study", "hierarchy", "concurrency", "performance"],
    "A request handler on the hot path calls log.debug(\"cart \" + cart + \" for user \" + user) fifty times per request. DEBUG is switched off in production, so nothing is written — yet the service still burns measurable CPU and allocates megabytes per second on that line, and when you switch DEBUG on to investigate an incident, latency doubles and the log file interleaves lines from twelve threads into nonsense. Design a Logging Framework that survives both states.",
    "Two mechanisms carry this design. The first is the LOGGER NAME HIERARCHY: loggers are named by dotted package path, an unconfigured logger INHERITS its level from the nearest configured ancestor, and the resolved level is cached so isEnabled() costs one integer comparison — which is what lets a disabled log call be nearly free, provided the framework is asked BEFORE the arguments are built. The second is the ASYNC APPENDER: a bounded ring buffer plus a single drain thread moves formatting and I/O off the caller, and the entire interview then lives in one question — what happens when that buffer fills, block the application or drop the logs?",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: application code obtains a LOGGER by name (conventionally the fully-qualified class name) and emits records at SEVERITY LEVELS — TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, FATAL — with a message, optional parameters, and an optional throwable. Configuration decides, per logger name, which levels are enabled and where output goes: console, a rolling file, a syslog or network sink, several at once. Output FORMAT is configurable (timestamp, level, logger name, thread, message, stack trace, correlation ids from a thread-local context map), and different destinations may format differently — a human-readable console layout plus a JSON layout for the log shipper from the SAME record. Levels must be reconfigurable AT RUNTIME so an on-call engineer can turn on DEBUG for one package without a restart. Non-functional requirements are the entire difficulty: a DISABLED log statement must cost approximately nothing — no string concatenation, no boxing, ideally one field read and one comparison, because logging calls sit inside the hottest loops in the system; an ENABLED statement must not block the caller on disk or network I/O; the framework must be THREAD-SAFE with interleaving-free records (a line from thread A never splices into thread B's line) and a defined ordering story; it must never lose the last records before a crash or a normal shutdown, since those are the ones that explain the incident; and it must never take the application down — a full disk or an unreachable log server is not a reason to fail a customer request. Clarify scope: single process, pluggable appenders, configuration loaded from a file plus a runtime API."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "LEVEL is an ordered enum whose ordinal IS the comparison (TRACE=0 ... FATAL=5), so 'is this enabled' is record.level >= effectiveLevel — an int compare, deliberately not a set lookup or a string match. LOGRECORD (or LogEvent) is the immutable value object capturing everything AT THE CALL SITE: level, logger name, raw message pattern plus argument array, timestamp, thread name, throwable, and a snapshot of the diagnostic CONTEXT MAP (MDC — request id, user id) — capturing these at call time rather than at write time is essential once appending is asynchronous, because by the time the drain thread formats the record the thread name and MDC belong to a different request. LOGGER holds a name, an OPTIONAL configured level (null means inherit), its own appender list, an ADDITIVITY flag (do records also travel to ancestors' appenders?), and a cached effectiveLevel. LOGGERFACTORY / LOGGERCONTEXT is the registry: a concurrent map from name to Logger, the place where the parent chain is wired and where a configuration change invalidates cached effective levels. APPENDER is the output Strategy — append(LogRecord) — with ConsoleAppender, FileAppender (plus rolling policy by size or time), and AsyncAppender, which is a DECORATOR wrapping another appender rather than a destination of its own. LAYOUT/FORMATTER turns a record into bytes and belongs to the appender, not the logger, so the same record renders as text on console and JSON in a file. FILTER is an optional per-appender predicate (level ranges, marker matching, sampling). RINGBUFFER inside AsyncAppender is the bounded handoff queue with an explicit full-policy."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The class graph is small; the design content is in the RELATIONSHIPS. Loggers form a TREE by name: 'com.shop.checkout.PaymentService' is a child of 'com.shop.checkout', which is a child of 'com.shop', which is a child of ROOT. Only ROOT is required to have a level; everyone else may be unset and inherits. This is the Chain of Responsibility applied to configuration lookup, and it is what makes 'set com.shop.checkout to DEBUG' affect a hundred classes with one line. APPENDER is Strategy — one append() contract, many destinations — and AsyncAppender is DECORATOR over that same interface, which is why asynchrony is a configuration choice rather than a rewrite: wrap the file appender and the call site does not change. LAYOUT is a second Strategy owned by the appender. The FACTORY (LoggerFactory.getLogger) exists so logger identity is canonical per name — two classes asking for the same name get the same object, which matters because configuration mutates that object. Deliberate omissions worth stating: no Singleton logger (a single global logger destroys per-package configuration, the framework's main feature); no inheritance-based Logger subclasses per level (levels are data, not types); no synchronized on the logger itself (the logger is read-mostly — level is volatile, appender list is copy-on-write — so the hot path takes no lock at all). The APPEND path is where thread-safety is concentrated: each appender is responsible for its own atomicity, so FileAppender serializes writes to its stream and the async one hands off to a lock-free or lock-guarded queue. And the single most important API decision: the framework exposes isEnabled(level) publicly and offers PARAMETERIZED messages — log.debug(\"cart {} user {}\", cart, user) — so that argument formatting happens only after the level check passes."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "enum Level { TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR, FATAL, OFF }\n\nfinal class LogRecord {                      // captured at the CALL SITE, immutable\n  final Level level; final String logger, pattern; final Object[] args;\n  final long timestampMs; final String thread; final Throwable error;\n  final Map<String, String> mdc;             // snapshot: async formatting must not re-read\n  LogRecord(Level l, String lg, String p, Object[] a, Throwable t) {\n    level = l; logger = lg; pattern = p; args = a; error = t;\n    timestampMs = System.currentTimeMillis();\n    thread = Thread.currentThread().getName();\n    mdc = MDC.snapshot();\n  }\n}\n\nclass Logger {\n  final String name; final Logger parent;\n  private volatile Level configured;              // null = inherit from parent\n  private volatile Level effective;               // cached resolution, recomputed on config change\n  private volatile boolean additive = true;\n  private final CopyOnWriteArrayList<Appender> appenders = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();\n\n  Level effectiveLevel() {                        // hierarchy walk, done once and cached\n    Level e = effective;\n    if (e != null) return e;\n    for (Logger l = this; l != null; l = l.parent)\n      if (l.configured != null) { effective = l.configured; return l.configured; }\n    effective = Level.INFO; return Level.INFO;    // ROOT default\n  }\n\n  public boolean isEnabled(Level lv) {            // THE hot-path check: one int compare\n    return lv.ordinal() >= effectiveLevel().ordinal();\n  }\n\n  public void debug(String pattern, Object... args) {\n    if (!isEnabled(Level.DEBUG)) return;          // guard BEFORE any formatting\n    dispatch(new LogRecord(Level.DEBUG, name, pattern, args, null));\n  }\n\n  private void dispatch(LogRecord r) {            // walk up while additivity holds\n    for (Logger l = this; l != null; l = l.parent) {\n      for (Appender a : l.appenders) a.append(r);\n      if (!l.additive) break;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\ninterface Appender { void append(LogRecord r); void flush(); void close(); }\n\n/** DECORATOR: makes any appender asynchronous via a bounded ring buffer. */\nclass AsyncAppender implements Appender {\n  private final Appender delegate;\n  private final ArrayBlockingQueue<LogRecord> ring;\n  private final boolean blockWhenFull;            // back-pressure vs drop: the policy knob\n  private final Thread drain;\n  private volatile boolean running = true;\n\n  AsyncAppender(Appender delegate, int capacity, boolean blockWhenFull) {\n    this.delegate = delegate; this.blockWhenFull = blockWhenFull;\n    this.ring = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(capacity);\n    this.drain = new Thread(this::drainLoop, \"log-drain\");\n    drain.setDaemon(false);                       // non-daemon: JVM waits for the flush\n    drain.start();\n    Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(this::close));\n  }\n\n  public void append(LogRecord r) {\n    if (blockWhenFull) {\n      try { ring.put(r); }                        // back-pressure: caller slows down\n      catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n    } else if (!ring.offer(r)) {                  // drop policy: never stall the app\n      dropped.incrementAndGet();                  // but COUNT it — silent loss is the bug\n    }\n  }\n\n  private void drainLoop() {\n    while (running || !ring.isEmpty()) {\n      try {\n        LogRecord r = ring.poll(200, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n        if (r != null) delegate.append(r);        // ONE thread formats + writes = ordered\n      } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n    }\n    delegate.flush();\n  }\n\n  public void close() {                           // flush-on-shutdown: the records that matter\n    running = false;\n    try { drain.join(5_000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n    delegate.close();\n  }\n  private final AtomicLong dropped = new AtomicLong();\n  public void flush() { delegate.flush(); }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the logger hierarchy, level inheritance, and additivity",
        "The hierarchy is what turns configuration from a per-class chore into a two-line operational tool, and interviewers probe whether you understand its exact semantics. Logger names are DOT-SEPARATED PATHS, and parenthood is a pure string-prefix relation: 'com.shop' is the parent of 'com.shop.checkout'. When code asks for a logger the factory creates any missing ancestors as placeholder nodes so the chain is always complete. A logger's CONFIGURED level may be null; its EFFECTIVE level is the configured level of the nearest ancestor that has one, with ROOT guaranteeing a default. So setting com.shop.checkout=DEBUG turns on every class beneath it, and setting com.shop.checkout.PaymentService=WARN under it turns one noisy class back down — precision without touching code. ADDITIVITY is the second axis and the one people forget: a record is offered to the logger's own appenders and then, if additivity is true, to each ancestor's appenders in turn. That is why writing to a dedicated audit file usually ALSO writes to the console — the ROOT appender is still in the chain — and why setting additivity=false on the audit logger is the fix. Duplicate log lines in production are, nine times out of ten, an additivity misunderstanding. Now the performance point: walking the parent chain on every call would put a loop with pointer chases on the hot path, so the resolved level is CACHED in a volatile field and INVALIDATED when configuration changes. Invalidation is a subtree operation: changing com.shop's level must clear the cached effective level of every descendant, not just itself. Volatile is exactly the right primitive here — the hot path needs a cheap read with visibility, and a stale value for a few nanoseconds after a reconfiguration is harmless. The trap to avoid is a synchronized effectiveLevel(); logging is called from every thread in the process and a lock there is a global serialization point that shows up in flame graphs as the application's dominant cost."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the async appender — ring buffer, back-pressure vs drop, ordering, shutdown",
        "Writing to disk from the request thread means every log line pays a syscall and, on a slow or full disk, an unbounded stall — logging becomes an availability risk. The ASYNC APPENDER decouples: the caller enqueues a record into a BOUNDED ring buffer and returns; a dedicated drain thread formats and writes. Bounded is not an implementation detail — an unbounded queue converts a logging backlog into an OutOfMemoryError, which is a worse outage than slow logs, so the capacity is fixed and the design must answer what happens at the boundary. THE FULL-BUFFER POLICY is the interview's fork. BACK-PRESSURE (put(), blocking the caller) preserves every record and slows the application to the speed of the disk — correct for audit, billing, and security logs where a missing line is a compliance failure, dangerous for hot-path debug logging because a stalled logger now propagates latency into user requests and can cascade into thread-pool exhaustion. DROP (offer() returning false) protects application latency absolutely and loses records precisely when load is highest — which is exactly when you want them. The senior answer is neither dogma but SEGREGATION: separate appenders with separate policies — drop for DEBUG/INFO, block (or a bounded wait with a timeout) for ERROR and audit — plus an always-incremented DROPPED COUNTER emitted as a metric and a synthetic 'lost N records' line, because silent loss is what makes people distrust logs. ORDERING: a single drain thread gives a total order of records as they were ENQUEUED, which is not the same as the order the events occurred — two threads can build records in one order and enqueue in the reverse. That is why timestamp and thread are captured in the record's constructor, not at write time; consumers sort by timestamp, and a monotonic sequence number breaks ties within the same millisecond. Multiple drain threads would raise throughput and destroy ordering, so keep one per appender. SHUTDOWN is the last mile: a crash or a plain System.exit with records still in the buffer loses exactly the lines that explain the failure. Register a shutdown hook, make the drain thread NON-daemon so the JVM waits for it, set running=false, join with a bounded timeout, then flush and close the delegate — and note that a kill -9 defeats all of it, which is the honest limit and the argument for keeping the buffer small enough to drain quickly."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: why the level check must be cheap — and why parameterized messages exist",
        "Consider log.debug(\"cart \" + cart + \" for user \" + user) with DEBUG disabled. Java evaluates the ARGUMENT before the call: cart.toString() runs, user.toString() runs, a StringBuilder is allocated, the concatenation produces a String that is immediately garbage. Only THEN does debug() check the level and return without writing. On a path executed a million times a second this is pure waste: measurable CPU, gigabytes per hour of short-lived allocation, more frequent young-generation GC, and — worst — the cost is INVISIBLE, because nothing appears in the log to suggest a line you disabled is costing you anything. Three escalating fixes, and you should be able to rank them. (1) THE GUARD: if (log.isDebugEnabled()) log.debug(\"cart \" + cart + ...); the check is one volatile read and one int compare, and the expensive concatenation is never reached. Correct, but noisy at every call site. (2) PARAMETERIZED MESSAGES: log.debug(\"cart {} for user {}\", cart, user) passes the pattern and the arguments separately, so no String is built unless the record is actually emitted — this is the everyday answer, and it also gives structured logging a shot at recording the arguments as fields rather than baked-in text. Its residual cost is honest and worth naming: the varargs array is still allocated, and any argument requiring computation (list.stream()...collect(), an expensive toString) still runs. (3) SUPPLIERS/LAMBDAS: log.debug(() -> \"cart \" + expensive()) defers the computation entirely, at the price of a lambda capture — best where building the message is genuinely costly. The framework design consequence is that isEnabled must be a FIELD READ, not a map lookup by logger name, not a string comparison of level names, and not a synchronized method: hence the cached volatile effectiveLevel and the ordinal comparison. Two more hot-path rules follow: never build the LogRecord before the level check (its constructor snapshots time, thread, and MDC — none of that is free), and never let a disabled call touch the appender list. State the measurement discipline too: 'nearly free' means a few nanoseconds; if a profile shows logging in the top frames, it is almost always a disabled statement doing eager argument work or a lock inside the hot path."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "SYNC vs ASYNC APPENDING: synchronous is simple, ordered, and loses nothing on a clean crash, but couples request latency to disk; async decouples at the cost of a bounded loss window and a shutdown protocol. Most systems run async for volume logs and sync for audit — configuration, not a rewrite, because AsyncAppender is a decorator. BLOCK vs DROP on a full buffer: block preserves completeness and can propagate stalls into user-facing latency; drop preserves latency and loses records under exactly the load you care about. Segregate by severity and always count drops. HIERARCHICAL NAMED LOGGERS vs FLAT CATEGORIES vs MARKERS/TAGS: the dotted hierarchy maps to package structure and gives inheritance for free, but it cannot express cross-cutting concerns ('all security-relevant logs' spans many packages) — that is what MARKERS and filters exist for, and modern designs support both axes. TEXT vs STRUCTURED (JSON) LAYOUT: text is human-readable in a terminal, JSON is queryable in a log platform; the layout Strategy means both can attach to the same record, which is the argument for keeping the record a structured object until the very last step rather than formatting into a String at the call site. THREAD-LOCAL MDC is what makes logs correlatable across a request, and it comes with a real hazard — a value left behind in a pooled thread leaks into the next request's logs, so MDC must be cleared in a finally, and any async handoff must PROPAGATE the snapshot explicitly (which is why the record captures it). WHAT BREAKS AT SCALE: file rolling by size/time plus compression and retention becomes mandatory; the sink moves off-box (syslog, a shipper agent tailing the file) and the network-appender question repeats the block-vs-drop trade one level out; SAMPLING (log 1 in N of a repetitive event) and rate-limiting filters become the only way to keep volume affordable. Extension points: filters as pluggable predicates, per-appender level thresholds, dynamic reconfiguration via a management endpoint, and a compatibility facade so application code binds to an interface and the implementation is swappable."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'DEBUG is off — why is this line still expensive?' — argument evaluation happens before the call; use a guard, parameterized {} messages, or a supplier, and make isEnabled a cached volatile int compare. 'How does setting one package to DEBUG affect a hundred classes?' — the dotted logger hierarchy with level inheritance from the nearest configured ancestor, cached as effectiveLevel and invalidated across the subtree on reconfiguration. 'Why am I seeing every line twice?' — additivity: the record travels to ancestor appenders as well as the logger's own; set additivity=false on the dedicated logger. 'The async buffer is full — do you block or drop?' — neither universally: drop for DEBUG/INFO with a counted, reported loss; block or bounded-wait for ERROR and audit; never make the queue unbounded, because that trades slow logs for an OOM. 'Are async logs ordered?' — a single drain thread preserves ENQUEUE order; the record's timestamp and thread are captured at the call site precisely because write time is later and on a different thread, and a sequence number breaks same-millisecond ties. 'What about the last records before a crash?' — shutdown hook, non-daemon drain thread, running=false, bounded join, flush and close; kill -9 still loses the buffer, which argues for a modest capacity. 'Why is MDC in the record instead of read by the appender?' — by the time the drain thread formats it, the thread-local belongs to another request; capture the snapshot at construction. 'How do you make it thread-safe without locks on the hot path?' — volatile level, copy-on-write appender lists, immutable records, and confine mutation to the appender's own critical section. 'How does this evolve?' — layouts become JSON for a log platform, appenders gain network sinks with their own backpressure policy, and sampling filters cap volume; the class graph does not change, which is the point of Strategy plus Decorator."),
    ],
    "A logging framework is the LLD question that quietly tests systems judgement rather than pattern vocabulary. The hierarchy question (dotted names, level inheritance, additivity) checks whether you have configured a real system or only called log.info(). The async appender question is a concurrency design in miniature — bounded queue, single-consumer ordering, an explicit and defensible full-buffer policy, and a flush-on-shutdown protocol — and the interviewer will push on block-versus-drop until you segregate by severity and admit that silent loss must at least be counted. Finally the cheap-level-check question separates people who have profiled a hot path from people who have not: disabled statements still evaluate their arguments, which is why isEnabled guards, {} parameterization, and supplier overloads exist at all.",
    [
      G("eager_argument_construction", "Building the log message with string concatenation at the call site, so a disabled DEBUG statement still runs toString(), allocates, and produces garbage on the hot path.", "Guard with isDebugEnabled(), or pass a parameterized pattern with {} placeholders (or a supplier for genuinely costly messages) so formatting happens only after the level check — and make that check a cached volatile int compare.", "log.debug(\"x=\" + obj + \" y=\" + other); profiles showing StringBuilder allocation under a disabled logger; isEnabled implemented as a map lookup or a synchronized method."),
      G("unbounded_or_silent_async_queue", "Making the async appender's queue unbounded, or dropping records on a full bounded queue with no counter, no metric, and no severity distinction.", "Bound the ring buffer; choose the full-policy per severity — drop DEBUG/INFO (always incrementing a dropped counter and emitting a synthetic loss line), block or bounded-wait for ERROR and audit — and never let logging OOM the process.", "new LinkedBlockingQueue<>() with no capacity for log records; offer() whose false return is ignored; memory growth during a disk stall; no 'records dropped' metric anywhere."),
      G("write_time_context_capture", "Formatting the record on the drain thread by reading Thread.currentThread().getName(), the clock, or the MDC at write time instead of capturing them when the call was made.", "Capture level, timestamp, thread name, throwable, and an MDC snapshot in the immutable LogRecord at the call site; the drain thread must only format what it was handed, and consumers sort by that timestamp.", "log lines attributed to 'log-drain' instead of the request thread; request ids missing or belonging to a different request; timestamps clustered at flush boundaries rather than at event time."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.logging, "Logging framework class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── ATM ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m8", "lld_m8_t1", 1, "Design an ATM", "design-atm",
    ["case-study", "state-machine", "algorithm", "reliability"],
    "A customer asks for 3,700. The machine holds 2000-notes, 500-notes, 200-notes and 100-notes in four separate cassettes with real, finite counts — and the 500 cassette is empty. Greedy gives 2000 + 1000 in 500s that do not exist. Meanwhile the account has already been debited and the power flickers mid-dispense. Design the ATM: the state machine, the note-selection algorithm, and the crash-safety protocol.",
    "The ATM is a STATE MACHINE with hardware attached, and treating it as one is what stops the boolean soup (isCardIn, isAuthenticated, isDispensing) that every naive version becomes — each state object answers 'what is legal right now', and illegal operations are rejected structurally rather than by a forgotten if. The two genuinely hard mechanisms sit inside it: DENOMINATION SELECTION is bounded coin-change against actual cassette counts, where greedy is provably wrong and must be replaced by a bounded DP or backtracking search that runs BEFORE any money moves; and the DEBIT-THEN-DISPENSE window is a distributed-transaction problem in a shoebox — a journal, a reserve/confirm protocol, and reconciliation are what stand between a power cut and a customer charged for cash they never received.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a customer inserts a card, is authenticated by PIN with a retry limit (three attempts, then the card is retained or ejected per policy), and then performs one or more transactions in a session: WITHDRAW cash, DEPOSIT cash or a cheque, check BALANCE, TRANSFER between linked accounts, and change PIN. Withdrawal must respect the account balance and overdraft rules, a per-transaction cap, a daily cumulative limit, and the machine's own available cash; the amount must be dispensable from the notes physically present. The machine prints or emails a receipt, ejects the card, and returns to idle after a timeout at every step. Hardware components — card reader, keypad, screen, cash dispenser with multiple cassettes, deposit slot, printer — are devices with their own failure modes. Non-functional constraints define the design: CORRECTNESS OF MONEY is absolute, so no interleaving of failures may leave the ledger and the cash drawer disagreeing without a recorded, reconcilable trace; the machine must be CRASH-SAFE — power loss, network partition to the bank, or a jammed dispenser can happen at any instant, including between debit and dispense; the session must be SECURE (PIN never stored in cleartext, card retained on repeated failure, screen timeouts); and the machine is effectively SINGLE-USER — one customer at a time — which mercifully removes intra-machine concurrency but replaces it with the harder problem of concurrency against the BANK, where the same account may be hit from another ATM, a POS terminal, or a phone app simultaneously. Clarify scope: one ATM's software, the bank behind a service interface, hardware behind device interfaces."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "ATM is the CONTEXT: it holds references to the devices, the current ATMState, the current Session, and the cash inventory; every user action is forwarded to the state object. ATMSTATE is the state interface — insertCard, enterPin, selectTransaction, enterAmount, cancel, timeout — implemented by IdleState, CardInsertedState, AuthenticatedState, TransactionSelectedState, DispensingState and OutOfServiceState; each implements only what is legal and returns a rejection for the rest. CARD is a value object (number, expiry, and for the ATM's purposes an opaque token, never storing the PIN). SESSION holds the authenticated customer, the selected account, the start time for timeout enforcement, and the running per-session totals for limit checks. TRANSACTION is the record with its own small lifecycle — CREATED, AUTHORIZED, DISPENSING, COMPLETED, FAILED, REVERSED — plus an id used as the IDEMPOTENCY KEY against the bank. CASHCASSETTE is the physical unit: a denomination and a mutable note count, with reserve/commit operations rather than a raw setter. CASHDISPENSER owns the cassette list and the one interesting algorithm — plan(amount) returning a note plan or reporting infeasibility — and the physical dispense(plan) that decrements counts as notes actually leave. BANKSERVICE is the network boundary: authenticate(card, pin), balance(account), and the money operations expressed as hold/debit/confirm/reverse with an idempotency key. JOURNAL is an append-only, flush-before-act write-ahead log of every state transition and every money-affecting intent — the component that turns 'we crashed' from an unanswerable question into a recovery procedure. DEVICE interfaces (CardReader, Printer, DepositSlot) abstract hardware for testability."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "STATE is the load-bearing pattern and the reason to reach for it is concrete: the legal operations differ per phase, and encoding that with flags produces methods that begin with four guard clauses each, drift apart, and eventually allow a withdrawal from a machine with no card in it. With State, ATM.enterPin() simply delegates to state.enterPin(this, pin); IdleState's implementation returns 'no card' and AuthenticatedState does not implement enterPin at all — illegality is structural. Transitions live inside the state objects (or in one transition table if the interviewer prefers explicitness), so the diagram on the whiteboard and the code are the same artifact. Say what State buys over an enum-plus-switch: the switch version puts every phase's logic in one growing method and recompiles the whole machine to add a state, while the object version adds a class; the enum version is defensible for three states and stops being defensible here, at six-plus with device interactions. Supporting patterns, each justified: STRATEGY for the note-selection algorithm, because 'minimum notes' and 'balance the cassettes' and 'prefer smaller notes for small amounts' are genuinely different policies a bank tunes per site; CHAIN OF RESPONSIBILITY or a simple validator list for the withdrawal preconditions (card valid, session limit, daily limit, account balance, machine capability) so each rule is independently testable and the rejection reason is precise; ADAPTER for every hardware device so the whole machine runs against simulated devices in tests — an ATM you cannot test without a cash drawer is undeployable; and OBSERVER for the audit/journal listeners so logging is not smeared through the transaction code. One firm architectural rule: THE DISPENSER NEVER TALKS TO THE BANK AND THE BANK CLIENT NEVER TOUCHES CASSETTES. The transaction coordinator sequences them, which is what makes the crash protocol expressible in one place."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "interface ATMState {\n  default void insertCard(ATM atm, Card c)      { atm.display(\"Not available now\"); }\n  default void enterPin(ATM atm, String pin)    { atm.display(\"Not available now\"); }\n  default void withdraw(ATM atm, long amount)   { atm.display(\"Not available now\"); }\n  default void cancel(ATM atm)                  { atm.ejectCard(); atm.setState(new IdleState()); }\n}\n\nclass IdleState implements ATMState {\n  public void insertCard(ATM atm, Card c) {\n    if (!atm.cardReader().read(c)) { atm.ejectCard(); return; }\n    atm.startSession(c);\n    atm.setState(new CardInsertedState(0));      // pin attempts so far\n  }\n}\n\nclass CardInsertedState implements ATMState {\n  private final int attempts;\n  CardInsertedState(int attempts) { this.attempts = attempts; }\n  public void enterPin(ATM atm, String pin) {\n    if (atm.bank().authenticate(atm.session().card(), pin)) {\n      atm.setState(new AuthenticatedState());\n    } else if (attempts + 1 >= 3) {\n      atm.retainCard();                          // policy: retain after 3 failures\n      atm.setState(new IdleState());\n    } else {\n      atm.display(\"Incorrect PIN\");\n      atm.setState(new CardInsertedState(attempts + 1));\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** Bounded coin change over REAL cassette counts. Greedy is wrong; this is not. */\nclass CashDispenser {\n  private final List<Cassette> cassettes;        // sorted desc by denomination\n\n  Optional<Map<Integer, Integer>> plan(long amount) {\n    int n = (int) amount;\n    int[] best = new int[n + 1];                 // best[v] = min notes to make v\n    int[] pick = new int[n + 1];                 // denomination chosen at v\n    Arrays.fill(best, Integer.MAX_VALUE); best[0] = 0;\n    for (Cassette c : cassettes) {               // bounded knapsack: count-limited\n      int d = c.denomination();\n      for (int used = 1; used <= c.count(); used++)\n        for (int v = n; v >= d; v--)\n          if (best[v - d] != Integer.MAX_VALUE && best[v - d] + 1 < best[v]) {\n            best[v] = best[v - d] + 1; pick[v] = d;\n          }\n    }\n    if (best[n] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) return Optional.empty();   // NOT dispensable\n    Map<Integer, Integer> notes = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n    for (int v = n; v > 0; v -= pick[v]) notes.merge(pick[v], 1, Integer::sum);\n    return Optional.of(notes);\n  }\n\n  synchronized void dispense(Map<Integer, Integer> notes) {\n    for (var e : notes.entrySet()) cassetteFor(e.getKey()).take(e.getValue());\n  }\n}\n\nclass WithdrawalCoordinator {\n  private final BankService bank; private final CashDispenser dispenser; private final Journal journal;\n\n  void withdraw(Session s, long amount) {\n    Transaction tx = Transaction.create(s, amount);                 // stable id = idempotency key\n    var plan = dispenser.plan(amount);\n    if (plan.isEmpty()) { s.display(\"Amount not dispensable — try a multiple of 100\"); return; }\n\n    journal.append(tx.id(), \"INTENT\", amount, plan.get());          // WAL: flushed before acting\n    if (!bank.hold(tx.id(), s.account(), amount)) {                 // idempotent by tx id\n      journal.append(tx.id(), \"DECLINED\", amount, null); return;\n    }\n    journal.append(tx.id(), \"HELD\", amount, null);\n    try {\n      dispenser.dispense(plan.get());                               // physical, irreversible\n      journal.append(tx.id(), \"DISPENSED\", amount, plan.get());\n      bank.confirm(tx.id());                                        // hold -> real debit\n      journal.append(tx.id(), \"CONFIRMED\", amount, null);\n    } catch (DispenseException e) {\n      journal.append(tx.id(), \"DISPENSE_FAILED\", e.dispensed(), null);\n      bank.reverse(tx.id(), amount - e.dispensed());                // refund the undelivered part\n    }\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the transaction state machine and why flags lose",
        "Sketch the machine explicitly, because interviewers grade whether you can enumerate states and transitions without hand-waving. IDLE accepts only insertCard. CARD_INSERTED accepts enterPin and cancel; it carries the attempt counter, and three failures move to card retention and back to IDLE. AUTHENTICATED accepts transaction selection, balance query, and cancel. AMOUNT_ENTRY accepts an amount or cancel. DISPENSING accepts NOTHING from the user — this is the critical one; cancel must be refused once notes are in motion, and a design that lets a user press Cancel mid-dispense has invented a way to be handed cash without being debited. COMPLETED offers another transaction or eject. OUT_OF_SERVICE is reachable from anywhere on a hardware fault and accepts nothing but an operator key. Layer on the cross-cutting transitions people forget: a TIMEOUT at every interactive state that ejects the card and returns to IDLE (an abandoned session with an authenticated card is a security hole), and a HARDWARE FAULT edge from any state. Now the argument for State objects over booleans. The flag version — if (cardInserted && authenticated && !dispensing) — has 2^n reachable combinations of which only a handful are legal, and every new feature adds a flag and doubles the illegal space; the bugs that result are precisely the ones that matter (a withdrawal accepted while the previous dispense is still running). The State version makes the illegal states UNREPRESENTABLE: there is no object on which both enterPin and withdraw are meaningful. Two refinements worth volunteering: keep the state objects STATELESS where possible and put session data on the ATM/Session (so states can be shared instances), and note that transaction state — CREATED/AUTHORIZED/DISPENSING/COMPLETED/REVERSED — is a SECOND, independent machine that outlives a session and is the one the recovery routine reads. Confusing the UI state machine with the transaction state machine is a common structural mistake; they have different lifetimes and different persistence requirements."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: denomination selection is bounded coin change, not greedy",
        "Ask for 3,700 from cassettes holding {2000: 5, 500: 0, 200: 3, 100: 20}. Greedy takes one 2000, then wants three 500s and finds an empty cassette, then... backtracks badly or fails. Yet the amount IS dispensable: 2000 + 200×3 + 100×11 = 3,700. Greedy is only optimal for CANONICAL coin systems with unlimited supply; ATM cassettes are neither — supply is bounded and a cassette can be empty, which breaks the canonical property immediately. So the correct framing is BOUNDED COIN CHANGE (a bounded knapsack): given denominations with per-denomination counts, find a multiset summing to the amount, minimising the number of notes. The DP in the code is O(amount × total notes) over an amount capped by the withdrawal limit and note counts in the low thousands, so it runs in microseconds — this is not a place where an exponential search is needed, and saying that shows you sized the problem rather than reaching for the biggest hammer. Two implementation details earn credit. First, RECONSTRUCTION: keep a pick[] array so you can produce the actual note plan, not just the count — the machine must know what to dispense. Second, INFEASIBILITY IS A FIRST-CLASS ANSWER: plan() returns empty when best[amount] is unreachable, and the correct product behaviour is a specific message ('try a multiple of 100' or 'maximum 3,600 available in this machine') rather than a generic failure. The sequencing rule matters as much as the algorithm: PLAN BEFORE YOU DEBIT. A design that authorizes the bank first and then discovers the notes cannot be assembled has created a reversal for no reason, and reversals are the operationally expensive path. Then note the policy layer: 'minimum notes' is only one objective. Real banks tune for CASSETTE BALANCING (avoid draining the 100s so the machine stays useful for small withdrawals), for a preferred mix (at least one small note for a 4,600 request), or for note-quality routing. That variability is exactly why the selection algorithm is a STRATEGY behind plan(amount) rather than a hardcoded loop — and the DP generalizes by changing the cost function from note count to a weighted objective. Finally, the reservation subtlety: the plan is computed from counts that must not change underneath it. On a single-user machine that is trivially safe; the moment a maintenance refill or a second transaction can interleave, plan-then-dispense becomes check-then-act and needs the cassette counts reserved (decrement on plan acceptance, release on failure) under the dispenser's lock."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: crash safety — the debit/dispense window, journaling, and reconciliation",
        "Two irreversible actions sit in one operation: debiting an account in a remote ledger and physically pushing notes out of a slot. There is no atomic commit across them — this is a two-phase problem with one participant that cannot be rolled back (cash in a customer's hand). Enumerate the orders. DISPENSE FIRST, DEBIT AFTER: if the network dies after the notes are out, the customer keeps money the bank never charged — the bank eats the loss, so no bank does this. DEBIT FIRST, DISPENSE AFTER: if the machine loses power after the debit and before or during the dispense, the customer is charged for cash they never received — recoverable, since the ledger can be reversed, but only if the machine RECORDED ITS INTENT. That is why every real ATM does debit-first with a WRITE-AHEAD JOURNAL: append INTENT (transaction id, account, amount, note plan) and FLUSH IT TO DURABLE STORAGE BEFORE calling the bank, then append after each subsequent step. On restart, the machine reads the journal, finds any transaction not in a terminal state, and drives a RECOVERY procedure: if the last record is INTENT with no HELD, query the bank by transaction id (idempotency in action — the bank can answer 'I never saw it' or 'I held it'); if HELD but not DISPENSED, reverse the hold; if DISPENSED but not CONFIRMED, confirm, because the cash is gone and the ledger must match. The refinement that makes this tractable is HOLD/CONFIRM instead of a raw debit: bank.hold(txId, account, amount) reserves funds and is idempotent by txId, dispense happens, then confirm converts the hold to a debit — and an unconfirmed hold expires on the bank side, so a machine that never comes back does not permanently freeze the customer's money. PARTIAL DISPENSE is the case juniors omit entirely: the dispenser jams after ejecting 2,000 of 3,700. The exception must carry HOW MUCH physically left the machine, the journal must record it, and the reversal must be for the undelivered remainder only — which is why the dispenser counts notes as they pass the sensor rather than assuming the plan succeeded. The final safety net is RECONCILIATION: the machine's expected cash (starting count minus journaled dispenses) is compared against the physical count at every cash-collection visit, and a discrepancy triggers an investigation against the journal. State the honest limit — you cannot make cash and ledger atomic; you make every failure DETECTABLE and REPAIRABLE, and that reframing is the answer the interviewer is listening for."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "STATE OBJECTS vs ENUM + TRANSITION TABLE: objects localize behaviour and scale to many states with device interactions; an explicit table is more auditable and easier to visualize or verify formally, which regulated hardware sometimes prefers — pick objects for behaviour-heavy states, a table when the transition set itself is the specification. HOLD/CONFIRM vs DIRECT DEBIT + REVERSE: hold/confirm needs bank support and expiring reservations but leaves a clean, self-healing failure path; direct debit with compensating reversal works against simpler bank APIs at the cost of a customer visibly seeing money leave and return. MIN-NOTES vs CASSETTE-BALANCING dispensing: minimum notes is fastest and best for the customer's wallet; balancing keeps the machine serviceable longer between refills and reduces truck rolls — the objective is a business tuning knob, which is the argument for Strategy. SYNCHRONOUS BANK CALL vs STORE-AND-FORWARD: online authorization is correct but makes the machine useless during a network partition; some networks allow offline limits with deferred settlement, trading a bounded fraud exposure for availability — naming this trade-off signals domain awareness. DP PLANNING vs PRECOMPUTED TABLES: for a fixed set of denominations and the standard multiples, a cached plan table is faster, but it must be invalidated on every count change; the DP is fast enough that caching is premature. Extensions to expect: DEPOSITS (note validation, envelope vs recycling machines, and provisional credit that clears later — a whole second state machine); MULTI-CURRENCY (cassettes tagged by currency, plan() per currency, FX quoting as a separate service); CARDLESS withdrawal by one-time code (replaces the auth path, leaves the dispense protocol identical — a good test of whether your layering held); MAINTENANCE MODE with refill operations that reset counts through the journal so reconciliation stays valid; and predictive CASH FORECASTING, which is analytics on the journal rather than machine logic."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'The 500 cassette is empty and the customer wants 3,700 — what happens?' — plan() runs bounded coin change over the real counts, finds 2000 + 200×3 + 100×11, and dispenses that; greedy would fail on an amount that is perfectly dispensable. 'And if no combination exists?' — plan() returns empty, the machine says so specifically before touching the bank, and no reversal is ever needed. 'Power fails right after the debit.' — the journal has INTENT and HELD flushed before the bank call; on restart the recovery routine queries the bank by transaction id and either reverses the hold or confirms it if the journal shows DISPENSED. 'The dispenser jams halfway.' — the exception carries the notes actually ejected, the journal records the partial amount, and only the undelivered remainder is reversed — which requires counting notes at the sensor, not trusting the plan. 'Why not dispense first and debit after?' — a network failure then gives the customer free money with no recovery path; the bank always takes the recoverable side of the risk. 'Can the user cancel during dispensing?' — no: DispensingState accepts no user input, because a cancel that stops a debit already committed while notes are moving is a hole. 'Three wrong PINs.' — the attempt counter lives in the state object, and the third failure retains or ejects the card per policy and returns to IDLE. 'Two ATMs, same account, same second.' — intra-machine concurrency is not the issue; the bank enforces the balance and the daily limit atomically, keyed by account, and the ATM's idempotent transaction id is what makes its retries safe. 'How would you test it?' — every device is behind an interface, so the whole machine runs against fakes; the journal makes crash tests reproducible by killing the process at each recorded step and asserting the recovery outcome."),
    ],
    "The ATM is the classic 'do you know the State pattern' prompt, but everyone gets that part — differentiation comes from the two mechanisms most candidates skip. First, denomination selection: interviewers deliberately empty a cassette to see whether you notice that greedy is only optimal for canonical, unbounded coin systems and that ATM cassettes are neither, making this bounded coin change with an infeasibility answer. Second, the debit/dispense window: strong candidates volunteer the write-ahead journal, hold/confirm with an idempotent transaction id, partial-dispense accounting, restart recovery, and physical reconciliation — and can say plainly that cash and ledger cannot be made atomic, only reconcilable. Mentioning that DispensingState accepts no cancel, and that the UI state machine and the transaction state machine are separate, reads as having thought about the money rather than the menus.",
    [
      G("greedy_denomination", "Selecting notes greedily from the largest denomination down, ignoring that cassettes have finite counts and can be empty — so a dispensable amount is refused or a plan is produced for notes that do not exist.", "Model it as bounded coin change over the actual per-cassette counts (a bounded-knapsack DP with reconstruction), return an explicit infeasible result when no multiset sums to the amount, and run the plan BEFORE authorizing the bank.", "while (amount >= denom) { count++; amount -= denom; } with no count check; plan produced without consulting cassette.count(); 'insufficient funds in machine' for amounts that other note combinations cover."),
      G("no_journal_no_idempotency", "Debiting the account and then dispensing with no durable record of intent, no idempotency key on the bank call, and no accounting for a partial dispense.", "Append a flushed write-ahead journal entry before each irreversible step, use the transaction id as an idempotency key with hold/confirm/reverse semantics, count notes actually ejected, and run a restart recovery routine that drives every non-terminal transaction to a terminal state.", "bank.debit() followed by dispenser.dispense() with nothing persisted between them; retries that double-debit; DispenseException handled by a generic catch that logs and returns; no reconciliation between expected and physical cash."),
      G("flag_soup_state", "Tracking phase with booleans (cardInserted, authenticated, dispensing) and guarding each operation with ad-hoc if-chains, including allowing cancel during dispensing.", "Use State objects (or an explicit transition table) so each phase implements only its legal operations; put the attempt counter and timeouts in the states, make DispensingState reject all user input, and keep the transaction state machine separate from the UI one.", "if (isAuthenticated && !isDispensing) sprinkled through the controller; new features adding another boolean; a cancel path reachable after the bank hold succeeded; timeout handling in only some screens."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.atm, "ATM class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── CHESS ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m8", "lld_m8_t2", 2, "Design Chess", "design-chess",
    ["case-study", "polymorphism", "rules-engine", "state"],
    "Your move generator says the pinned knight on f3 may jump to e5. It may not — moving it exposes your own king to the bishop on b7, and chess has no rule that lets you make that move. Meanwhile a pawn on e5 wants to capture en passant, which is only legal because the opponent's d-pawn moved two squares LAST move — information that does not exist anywhere on the current board. Design Chess: piece movement without a giant switch, legal moves versus pseudo-legal, and the special moves that need history.",
    "Three mechanisms carry this design. Piece movement is POLYMORPHIC — each subclass emits its own moves from a direction-vector table, so there is never a switch on piece type and adding a fairy piece is one class. Legality is TWO-PHASE: generate PSEUDO-legal moves that respect only the piece's geometry, then filter by simulating each one and asking 'is my king attacked?' — pins and checks fall out of that filter instead of needing bespoke pin detection. And castling, en passant, promotion, threefold repetition and the fifty-move rule are all rules about the GAME'S HISTORY, not the current position, which is why MoveHistory (with castling rights, the en-passant square, and the halfmove clock) is a first-class entity rather than a log.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: a two-player game on an 8×8 board with the standard six piece types, alternating turns starting with White. The engine must accept a move only if it is LEGAL under FIDE rules: the piece's movement geometry, blocking by intervening pieces (except the knight), capture rules, and the overriding constraint that a move may never leave or place one's own king in check. It must support the three special moves — CASTLING kingside and queenside with all its preconditions, EN PASSANT capture, and pawn PROMOTION with a chosen piece — and must detect terminal states: checkmate, stalemate, the fifty-move rule, threefold repetition, and insufficient material, plus resignation and agreed draws. It should expose the set of legal moves for a square (so a UI can highlight), support UNDO/redo, and serialize a position (FEN) and a game (PGN-style move list). Non-functional requirements shape the classes more than they look: RULE CORRECTNESS is the product — a single illegal move accepted invalidates the game — so the design must make the rules checkable in one place rather than duplicated across UI and engine; EXTENSIBILITY matters because variants (Chess960, three-check, custom pieces) are the natural follow-up; and PERFORMANCE matters only if an AI opponent is in scope, at which point move generation and make/unmake are called millions of times and the representation choice starts to dominate. Clarify with the interviewer: are we building the rules engine only, or also an AI? The answer changes whether an object board is acceptable or bitboards are warranted — and asking is itself signal."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "BOARD owns an 8×8 array of squares (or a 64-entry piece array), plus the ability to place, remove, and query pieces, and — crucially — makeMove/unmakeMove, because legality checking requires simulation. SQUARE is a value object of file and rank; using a small immutable type rather than raw ints makes off-board checks a single method. PIECE is the abstract base: colour, a hasMoved flag or (better) reliance on history for castling rights, and the polymorphic pseudoLegalMoves(Board, Square) plus an attacks(Board, from, to) helper used by check detection. KING, QUEEN, ROOK, BISHOP, KNIGHT and PAWN are the subclasses; queen, rook and bishop share a SLIDING implementation parameterised by direction vectors, while king and knight share a STEPPING implementation — that shared machinery in the base class is what keeps six subclasses to a few lines each. MOVE is a rich value object, and its richness is the design's spine: from, to, the moving piece, the captured piece (which for en passant is NOT on the destination square), a MoveType (NORMAL, CAPTURE, CASTLE_KING, CASTLE_QUEEN, EN_PASSANT, PROMOTION), the promotion piece type, and the UNDO INFORMATION needed to restore prior state — previous castling rights, previous en-passant square, previous halfmove clock. MOVEHISTORY is the ordered list of played moves plus the derived per-position state; it answers 'was the h1 rook ever moved', 'did the last move double-push a pawn', and 'has this position occurred three times' (a hash-count map, Zobrist keys in a fast implementation). MOVEGENERATOR turns pseudo-legal into legal. GAME is the session: board, history, side to move, clocks, result, and the state transitions to CHECKMATE/STALEMATE/DRAW. PLAYER carries identity and colour."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The first design decision is POLYMORPHIC MOVE GENERATION, and you should name the alternative you are rejecting: a single generateMoves(piece) with switch (piece.type) — every new piece edits shared code, the method grows past readability, and variant support means forking it. Instead Piece declares pseudoLegalMoves and each subclass supplies its geometry. The elegance comes from noticing that the six pieces are really THREE behaviours: SLIDERS (rook: four orthogonal vectors; bishop: four diagonals; queen: all eight) that ray-cast along each vector until they hit the edge, an enemy piece (capture and stop), or a friendly piece (stop); STEPPERS (knight: eight fixed offsets; king: eight adjacent) that test one square per offset; and the PAWN, which is genuinely special — direction depends on colour, it moves forward but captures diagonally, has an optional double push from its start rank, promotes on the last rank, and captures en passant. So the base class holds slide(vectors) and step(offsets), each subclass is a vector table plus one call, and Pawn is the one honest special case. Do NOT use instanceof in the engine — the two places that legitimately need type identity are promotion (creating the chosen piece, a small FACTORY) and check detection, which can be expressed as 'does any enemy piece's pseudo-legal set contain the king's square', requiring no type test at all. The second decision is that MOVE IS AN OBJECT rather than a from/to pair: it carries its own type and undo information, which is what makes unmakeMove exact and what lets special moves execute through one apply path. Patterns worth naming: STRATEGY-by-subclass for movement, COMMAND for Move (execute/undo — the natural undo/redo mechanism), MEMENTO for the position snapshot if you prefer copying over undo info, FACTORY for promotion piece creation, and OBSERVER for UI updates on state change. Avoid a rules class per piece that duplicates the piece hierarchy — one hierarchy, not two parallel ones."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "abstract class Piece {\n  final Color color;\n  Piece(Color c) { color = c; }\n  /** Geometry only: ignores whether the mover's own king ends up in check. */\n  abstract List<Move> pseudoLegalMoves(Board b, Square from);\n\n  protected List<Move> slide(Board b, Square from, int[][] vectors) {\n    List<Move> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int[] v : vectors) {\n      Square s = from.shift(v[0], v[1]);\n      while (s.onBoard()) {\n        Piece p = b.at(s);\n        if (p == null) { out.add(Move.normal(from, s, this)); s = s.shift(v[0], v[1]); continue; }\n        if (p.color != color) out.add(Move.capture(from, s, this, p));   // capture, then stop\n        break;                                                           // blocked either way\n      }\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n  protected List<Move> step(Board b, Square from, int[][] offsets) {\n    List<Move> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (int[] o : offsets) {\n      Square s = from.shift(o[0], o[1]);\n      if (!s.onBoard()) continue;\n      Piece p = b.at(s);\n      if (p == null) out.add(Move.normal(from, s, this));\n      else if (p.color != color) out.add(Move.capture(from, s, this, p));\n    }\n    return out;\n  }\n}\n\nclass Rook extends Piece {\n  private static final int[][] V = {{1,0},{-1,0},{0,1},{0,-1}};\n  Rook(Color c) { super(c); }\n  List<Move> pseudoLegalMoves(Board b, Square f) { return slide(b, f, V); }\n}\nclass Knight extends Piece {\n  private static final int[][] O = {{1,2},{2,1},{2,-1},{1,-2},{-1,-2},{-2,-1},{-2,1},{-1,2}};\n  Knight(Color c) { super(c); }\n  List<Move> pseudoLegalMoves(Board b, Square f) { return step(b, f, O); }\n}\n// Queen = slide over all 8 vectors; Bishop = 4 diagonals; King = step over all 8. No switch anywhere.\n\nclass MoveGenerator {\n  private final Board board; private final MoveHistory history;\n\n  /** THE filter: a pseudo-legal move is legal iff it does not leave your own king attacked. */\n  List<Move> legalMoves(Color side) {\n    List<Move> legal = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (Square from : board.squaresOccupiedBy(side)) {\n      List<Move> candidates = new ArrayList<>(board.at(from).pseudoLegalMoves(board, from));\n      candidates.addAll(specialMoves(from, side));        // castling, en passant, promotion\n      for (Move m : candidates) {\n        board.makeMove(m);                                // simulate\n        boolean selfCheck = isAttacked(board.kingSquare(side), side.opposite());\n        board.unmakeMove(m);                              // exact restore via m's undo info\n        if (!selfCheck) legal.add(m);                     // pins & checks fall out here\n      }\n    }\n    return legal;\n  }\n\n  boolean isAttacked(Square sq, Color by) {\n    for (Square from : board.squaresOccupiedBy(by))\n      for (Move m : board.at(from).pseudoLegalMoves(board, from))\n        if (m.to().equals(sq)) return true;\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  List<Move> specialMoves(Square from, Color side) {\n    List<Move> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    Piece p = board.at(from);\n    if (p instanceof Pawn) {\n      Move last = history.lastMove();                     // EN PASSANT needs HISTORY\n      if (last != null && last.isDoublePawnPush()\n          && last.to().rank() == from.rank()\n          && Math.abs(last.to().file() - from.file()) == 1)\n        out.add(Move.enPassant(from, last.to().shift(0, side.forward()), p, last.piece()));\n    }\n    if (p instanceof King && history.castlingRights(side).kingSide()\n        && board.emptyBetween(from, Square.of(7, from.rank()))\n        && !isAttacked(from, side.opposite())                              // not IN check\n        && !isAttacked(from.shift(1, 0), side.opposite())                  // not THROUGH check\n        && !isAttacked(from.shift(2, 0), side.opposite()))                 // not INTO check\n      out.add(Move.castleKingSide(from, p));\n    return out;\n  }\n\n  GameResult statusFor(Color side) {\n    boolean inCheck = isAttacked(board.kingSquare(side), side.opposite());\n    if (!legalMoves(side).isEmpty()) return GameResult.IN_PROGRESS;\n    return inCheck ? GameResult.checkmate(side.opposite()) : GameResult.STALEMATE;\n  }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: piece movement without a switch — vectors, sliders, and the pawn",
        "The instinct every junior brings is a central method that asks what kind of piece this is and branches. It is worth dissecting exactly why that decays. The branch body for a rook and for a bishop is the same ray-casting loop with different offsets, so the switch duplicates logic rather than separating it; the pawn's five special cases end up inside the same method as the knight's eight offsets; and the method becomes the single file every variant, every bug fix, and every new piece must touch, which in a team means constant merge conflicts on the most correctness-critical code in the system. Polymorphic dispatch replaces the branch with the type system: board.at(from).pseudoLegalMoves(board, from) — the JVM picks the implementation, and the engine never asks what the piece is. The second insight is that six subclasses do not mean six algorithms. SLIDING is one algorithm parameterised by a vector table: for each direction, walk until off-board or blocked, emitting quiet moves onto empty squares and a single capture onto the first enemy square, then stopping — the 'stop after capture' and 'stop before a friendly piece' rules are the whole of blocking, and getting them in ONE place means they cannot drift between rook and queen. STEPPING is the same shape without the loop. So Rook, Bishop, Queen, Knight and King are each a static vector array and a one-line call, which is the concrete payoff you should point at. THE PAWN is the honest exception and you should say so rather than contorting the abstraction: its direction depends on colour (a forward() on Color, not a branch), it moves to empty squares straight ahead but captures only diagonally (the one piece whose move and capture geometries differ), it may double-push only from its home rank and only through an empty intervening square, it promotes on reaching the last rank — which means the generator emits FOUR moves for one destination, one per promotion choice, a detail people forget until a UI has nowhere to ask the user — and it captures en passant, which needs history. Two anti-patterns to name: a parallel MoveValidator hierarchy mirroring the piece hierarchy (two hierarchies to keep in sync — collapse into one), and instanceof checks scattered through the engine (legitimate only at the promotion factory and, arguably, in special-move generation, and even there a boolean capability on Piece is cleaner)."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: pseudo-legal vs legal — the self-check filter, pins, checkmate and stalemate",
        "This is the distinction that separates a toy from a chess engine, and it is the single most probed idea in this question. A PSEUDO-LEGAL move respects only the piece's geometry and blocking. A LEGAL move additionally satisfies the global constraint: after the move, YOUR OWN KING MUST NOT BE ATTACKED. That constraint is global — it depends on every enemy piece's lines — so it cannot be expressed inside a piece's own movement logic, which is why the two-phase design exists. The naive attempt is to detect PINS up front: find pieces standing between your king and an enemy slider, and restrict them. This is a legitimate OPTIMIZATION but a poor CORRECTNESS strategy, because the cases multiply — a pinned piece may still move ALONG the pin ray (a pinned rook can capture the pinning bishop or slide toward it), en passant can expose a horizontal pin on the fifth rank by removing TWO pawns from one rank simultaneously (the classic edge case that breaks hand-rolled pin logic), and moving the king itself needs a different rule (it may not step onto an attacked square, including squares defended 'through' its own current position by a slider). The robust formulation is uniform and short: SIMULATE. make the move, ask isAttacked(kingSquare(me), opponent), unmake. Pins, discovered checks, moving out of check, blocking a check, and capturing the checking piece are ALL handled by that one filter, with no special cases — that generality is the argument. State the cost honestly: it is O(moves × attack-scan), roughly a few thousand operations per position, fine for a rules engine and a UI, too slow for a deep search, where engines add pin bitmasks, check-evasion generation, and incremental attack tables as OPTIMIZATIONS that must agree with the simple filter (which becomes the test oracle). unmakeMove must be EXACT, and this is where Move's undo information earns its keep: restoring the captured piece — remembering that en passant's captured pawn is not on the destination square — plus the previous castling rights, the previous en-passant square, and the previous halfmove clock. A make/unmake that restores the pieces but forgets castling rights produces a board that is right and a game that is wrong, and it will pass every casual test. Finally, terminal detection falls out for free and you should present it that way: no legal moves AND in check is CHECKMATE; no legal moves and NOT in check is STALEMATE, a draw. Deriving both from legalMoves().isEmpty() plus one attack query — rather than writing separate mate-detection logic — is the sign that the two-phase design was done right."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: castling, en passant, promotion — rules that need HISTORY, not the board",
        "A chess POSITION is not just the arrangement of pieces; FEN encodes four extra fields for exactly this reason, and if your design cannot answer why, it is incomplete. CASTLING requires: the king and the chosen rook have NEVER MOVED — a property of history, not of where they stand, since a rook can return to h1 and still have forfeited the right; the squares between them are empty; the king is not currently in check; the king does not PASS THROUGH an attacked square; and the king does not LAND on an attacked square. Three separate attack queries, and candidates routinely check only the destination. Note the asymmetry people miss: the ROOK may pass through an attacked square, and for queenside castling the b1 square must be empty but need not be safe. Model the right as CastlingRights (four booleans) carried in the game state and updated when a king or rook moves OR when a rook is captured on its home square — that last trigger is the one that gets forgotten. EN PASSANT is the purest history dependency in the game: a pawn may capture an enemy pawn that has just advanced two squares, capturing onto the square it skipped — and only IMMEDIATELY, on the very next move. Nothing on the board records that the pawn arrived by a double push; the position alone is ambiguous. So the state must carry either the last move or, as FEN does, an EN-PASSANT TARGET SQUARE that is set by a double push and cleared by every other move. Its implementation is also the one move where the captured piece is not on the destination square, which breaks any make/unmake that assumes 'captured = board.at(to)'. PROMOTION is history-light but structurally sharp: one source-destination pair yields four distinct legal moves, so the generator must emit all four and the Move must carry the chosen type; under-promotion to a knight is occasionally the only winning move, so hardcoding 'always queen' is a rules bug, not a simplification. Two more history rules complete the set: the FIFTY-MOVE RULE needs a halfmove clock reset by any pawn move or capture, and THREEFOLD REPETITION needs a count of previously-seen positions where 'position' means pieces PLUS side to move PLUS castling rights PLUS en-passant square — which is precisely why engines hash all four into a Zobrist key and keep a map of key to count. Every one of these is stored in MoveHistory or the game state and restored by unmakeMove, which is the concrete reason Move carries undo information rather than just coordinates."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "OBJECT BOARD vs BITBOARDS: an 8×8 array of Piece references is readable, debuggable, and the right answer for an LLD interview about a rules engine; bitboards (a 64-bit word per piece type per colour, with magic-bitboard sliding attacks) make move generation and attack queries orders of magnitude faster and are mandatory for a competitive AI, at the cost of unreadable code and no object model at all. Say which problem you are solving and pick accordingly — volunteering the bitboard option shows range without over-engineering the answer. MAKE/UNMAKE vs COPY-ON-MOVE (immutable board): make/unmake mutates and restores, which is fast and is what search needs, but demands exact undo information and is a bug farm if any state is forgotten; an immutable board returning a new position is trivially correct, thread-safe, and enables cheap history and repetition detection, at the cost of allocation per node — a fine trade for a UI-driven game, a fatal one inside a search tree. LEGAL-ONLY GENERATION vs GENERATE-THEN-FILTER: filtering is simple and uniform; generating only legal moves directly (using pin masks and check-evasion sets) is significantly faster and significantly harder to get right — keep the filter as the reference implementation and test the fast path against it. RULES IN PIECES vs A RULES ENGINE: putting geometry in the pieces and global constraints in the generator is the clean split; pushing everything into an external rules table buys configurability for variants at the price of indirection. Extensions to expect and how they land: CHESS960 changes only the starting position and the castling rule's encoding (rights become 'which rook file'), which is a good stress test of whether castling was modelled as history rather than as fixed squares; VARIANTS with new pieces are new Piece subclasses with new vector tables — one class, no engine edits, the payoff for refusing the switch; an AI OPPONENT slots behind a MoveChooser interface (minimax with alpha-beta over the same make/unmake), which is why make/unmake exists at all; TIME CONTROLS, draw offers, and resignation are Game-level state; and NETWORK PLAY needs move serialization plus server-side re-validation, since a client-side rules engine is advice, never authority."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Where is the switch on piece type?' — nowhere; each Piece subclass emits its own pseudo-legal moves, sliders and steppers share parameterised helpers in the base class, and the only legitimate type check is the promotion factory. 'A pinned knight — how do you stop it moving?' — you do not stop it specially; the pseudo-legal move is generated, simulated, and rejected because your king ends up attacked. Pins, discovered checks, and evasions all fall out of the same filter. 'Why not detect pins directly?' — as an optimization yes, as correctness no: a pinned piece may move along the pin ray, and en passant can expose a horizontal pin by removing two pawns from one rank at once. 'How do you know castling is still allowed?' — CastlingRights in the game state, updated when the king or rook moves and when a rook is captured on its home square; the board cannot tell you, because a rook can return home having lost the right. 'What are the castling checks?' — squares empty, king not in check, not through check, not into check; the rook may pass through an attacked square, and queenside needs b1 empty but not safe. 'En passant — where does the information live?' — the last move (or an en-passant target square) in the game state; also the only capture where the taken piece is not on the destination square, which any unmakeMove must handle. 'Checkmate vs stalemate?' — both are 'no legal moves'; in check makes it mate, not in check makes it stalemate. 'How do you detect threefold repetition?' — hash pieces plus side to move plus castling rights plus en-passant square (Zobrist) and count occurrences; positions that look identical but differ in rights are not repetitions. 'Undo?' — Move is a Command carrying its own undo information, so unmake restores pieces, captured piece, rights, ep square, and the halfmove clock exactly. 'Make it fast enough for an AI.' — keep this as the reference implementation, then add bitboards, incremental attack tables, and legal-only generation, testing each against the simple filter via perft node counts."),
    ],
    "Chess is the deepest pure-rules LLD question in common use, and it is graded on three specific things. First, whether piece movement is polymorphic — interviewers add a custom piece to see whether your answer is 'a new class' or 'another switch case'. Second, and decisively, whether you volunteer the pseudo-legal / legal distinction: candidates who try to encode pins and checks inside each piece's movement logic get buried by edge cases, while candidates who simulate and ask 'is my king attacked' get pins, discovered checks, evasions, checkmate and stalemate from one uniform mechanism. Third, whether you notice that castling rights, en passant, the halfmove clock and repetition are HISTORY, invisible in the current position — which is also why make/unmake needs exact undo information. Being able to say where bitboards would replace the object board, and why you would not start there, is the senior finish.",
    [
      G("switch_on_piece_type", "One generateMoves() method that branches on piece type, duplicating the ray-cast loop per sliding piece and mixing the pawn's special cases into the same body.", "Give Piece an abstract pseudoLegalMoves() and implement sliders and steppers once in the base class parameterised by direction vectors; each subclass becomes a vector table, and the pawn is the single honest special case.", "switch (piece.getType()) or a chain of instanceof inside the engine; near-identical loops for rook, bishop and queen; a parallel validator hierarchy mirroring the piece classes."),
      G("no_self_check_filter", "Treating geometrically valid moves as legal — or hand-coding pin detection inside each piece — so a pinned piece can move, or moving out of check is mishandled.", "Generate pseudo-legal moves, then filter: make the move, test isAttacked(ownKing, opponent), unmake. Pins, discovered checks, evasions, checkmate and stalemate all derive from that one simulation; treat pin masks as a later optimization validated against it.", "isValidMove implemented purely from geometry; separate bespoke logic for 'in check' move generation; a pinned piece able to move off the pin ray; checkmate detected by something other than 'no legal moves and in check'."),
      G("history_blind_special_moves", "Deriving castling, en passant and repetition from the current board alone — checking only that the king and rook stand on their home squares, or allowing en passant at any time.", "Carry castling rights, the en-passant target square, the halfmove clock and a position-hash count in the game state; update them on every move, restore them in unmakeMove, and clear castling rights when a rook is captured on its home square too.", "castling allowed after a rook moved away and returned; en passant legal more than one move later; unmakeMove restoring pieces but not rights or the ep square; repetition compared on piece placement only."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.chess, "Chess class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── STACK OVERFLOW ───────────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m8", "lld_m8_t3", 3, "Design Stack Overflow", "design-stack-overflow",
    ["case-study", "rules-engine", "inheritance", "policy"],
    "An answer gets upvoted (+10 to the author), then downvoted by someone else (−2 to the author, −1 to the voter), then accepted (+15), then the voter changes their mind and retracts the downvote, then the question is deleted. What is every affected user's reputation now — and where in your class diagram does that arithmetic live? Design Stack Overflow.",
    "The trap is scattering reputation arithmetic across the code that happens to trigger it — a +10 inside the vote handler, a +15 inside the accept handler, a −100 somewhere in moderation — after which nobody can answer 'why is my reputation 4,312' and every rule change is a hunt. The correct shape is a REPUTATION RULES ENGINE: votes and acceptances emit EVENTS, a data table maps (event type, affected role) to a delta, and one engine applies them with caps, floors, and exact REVERSAL when the event is undone. Around that sit two more separations: a Post hierarchy that shares votability and revision history without pretending a Comment is a Question, and PRIVILEGES as threshold policy objects checked in one place instead of `if (user.rep > 15)` sprinkled through controllers.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: registered USERS post QUESTIONS with a title, a body, and one to five TAGS; other users post ANSWERS to a question, and both questions and answers accept COMMENTS. Users VOTE posts up or down (one vote per user per post, changeable and retractable), the question's author may ACCEPT one answer, and everything is editable with a REVISION HISTORY. Reputation is earned and lost from these actions, and reputation unlocks PRIVILEGES progressively — comment anywhere, vote up, vote down, edit others' posts, close and reopen, access moderation tools. Users can FLAG content, and flags plus community close-votes drive moderation: close, reopen, delete, undelete, lock. BOUNTIES let a user stake reputation to attract answers. Search must find questions by free text and by tag, with filtering and ranking (relevance, votes, recency); tag pages and 'related questions' are the natural browse surfaces. BADGES are awarded for milestones. Non-functional constraints that shape the classes: the READ-TO-WRITE RATIO IS EXTREME — the overwhelming majority of traffic is anonymous readers of an existing question, so aggregates like score and answer count are DENORMALIZED onto the post while remaining derivable from the vote records; REPUTATION MUST BE EXPLAINABLE AND AUDITABLE, since users dispute it, which pushes you toward an event log rather than an in-place counter; RULES CHANGE — the exact deltas and caps are product decisions tuned over time, so they belong in configuration, not in code; and vote integrity matters, so one-vote-per-user-per-post must be enforced by a constraint, not by a check. Clarify scope: the domain model and its rules engine, storage behind repository interfaces, search behind an index interface."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "USER holds identity, reputation (a cached total plus a link to the event log that produces it), badge list, and per-day counters for capped events. POST is the abstract base for anything votable and commentable: id, author, body, createdAt, an edit/revision list, a comment list, a denormalized score, and a state (ACTIVE, CLOSED, DELETED, LOCKED). QUESTION extends Post with a title, a tag set, the answer list, an acceptedAnswerId, view count, and an optional bounty. ANSWER extends Post with a questionId and an accepted flag. COMMENT is deliberately NOT a full Post in most designs — it is short, has no answers, cannot be downvoted, and does not earn reputation; modelling it as a Post subclass drags in fields and behaviours that are meaningless for it, so most designs give it its own small type with an author, a body, and an upvote count, and say why. TAG is a first-class entity (name, description, synonyms, usage count) rather than a string, because tags carry moderation state and drive browse pages. VOTE is the record: (voterId, postId, VoteType) with a UNIQUE CONSTRAINT on (voterId, postId) — that constraint is the enforcement mechanism for one-vote-per-post, not an application-level check. REPUTATIONEVENT is the append-only ledger row: (userId, eventType, sourcePostId, delta, timestamp, reversedBy) — the audit trail that makes reputation explainable and reversible. REPUTATIONENGINE applies the rule table to events. PRIVILEGE is (name, threshold) with a checker that answers 'may this user do X'. BADGE is a rule evaluated against a user's history. SEARCHINDEX maps terms and tags to post ids with the metadata needed for ranking. MODERATIONSERVICE owns flags, close-votes, and the state transitions on Post."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The design is three separations, each defensible. FIRST, the POST HIERARCHY. Question and Answer genuinely share votability, comments, edits, ownership, and moderation state, so an abstract Post base is real reuse, not inheritance for its own sake — put score, author, body, revisions, and the state machine there, and keep the differences (title/tags/answers on Question, accepted on Answer) in the subclasses. The judgement call the interviewer is watching for is COMMENT: pulling it into the hierarchy makes the base carry 'can this be answered', 'can this be downvoted', 'does this earn reputation' as exceptions, so keep it separate and say the rule you applied — inherit shared BEHAVIOUR, not merely shared FIELDS. SECOND, REPUTATION AS AN EVENT-DRIVEN RULES ENGINE. Voting, accepting, flagging, and moderation all EMIT domain events; the engine holds a table of (eventType, role) to delta plus caps and floors, appends a ReputationEvent per affected user, and updates the cached total. Nothing outside the engine performs reputation arithmetic — that single rule is what keeps the numbers explainable. Implementationally this is a small Strategy/Command table rather than a pattern showpiece, and the honest framing is 'the rules are data'. THIRD, PRIVILEGES AS POLICY OBJECTS. A Privilege is a named threshold with a check(user) method, resolved through one PrivilegeService; controllers ask permissions.require(user, Privilege.DOWNVOTE) instead of comparing integers, so raising the downvote threshold is a config edit and moderator overrides live in one place. Supporting choices: OBSERVER for the event bus that connects voting to reputation, badges, and search indexing without the voting service knowing about any of them; STATE (or a guarded transition method) for the post lifecycle so 'delete a closed post' is decided once; and REPOSITORY interfaces so the aggregate boundaries — a Question with its answers is the natural aggregate for a page render — are explicit."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "abstract class Post {\n  final long id; final User author; String body;\n  final List<Revision> revisions = new ArrayList<>();\n  final List<Comment> comments = new ArrayList<>();\n  int score;                                   // denormalized; derivable from Vote records\n  PostState state = PostState.ACTIVE;          // ACTIVE, CLOSED, DELETED, LOCKED\n  Post(long id, User author, String body) { this.id = id; this.author = author; this.body = body; }\n  void edit(User editor, String newBody) {\n    revisions.add(new Revision(body, editor, Instant.now()));\n    body = newBody;\n  }\n}\nclass Question extends Post {\n  String title; final Set<Tag> tags; final List<Answer> answers = new ArrayList<>();\n  Long acceptedAnswerId; int views; Bounty bounty;\n}\nclass Answer extends Post {\n  final long questionId; boolean accepted;\n}\n// Comment is NOT a Post: no answers, no downvotes, no reputation. Inherit behaviour, not fields.\n\n/** Reputation rules are DATA. One engine applies them; nothing else does rep arithmetic. */\nenum RepEvent { UPVOTE_QUESTION, UPVOTE_ANSWER, DOWNVOTE_RECEIVED, DOWNVOTE_CAST,\n                ANSWER_ACCEPTED, ACCEPTER_BONUS, BOUNTY_AWARDED, POST_SPAM_FLAGGED }\n\nclass ReputationEngine {\n  private static final Map<RepEvent, Integer> DELTA = Map.of(\n      RepEvent.UPVOTE_QUESTION,    5,\n      RepEvent.UPVOTE_ANSWER,     10,\n      RepEvent.DOWNVOTE_RECEIVED, -2,\n      RepEvent.DOWNVOTE_CAST,     -1,\n      RepEvent.ANSWER_ACCEPTED,   15,\n      RepEvent.ACCEPTER_BONUS,     2,\n      RepEvent.POST_SPAM_FLAGGED, -100);\n  private static final int DAILY_CAP = 200;    // downvotes/accepts are exempt in the real rules\n  private final RepEventLog log;\n\n  /** Append-only: the total is a projection of the ledger, which is why it is explainable. */\n  void apply(RepEvent ev, User target, long sourcePostId) {\n    int delta = DELTA.getOrDefault(ev, 0);\n    if (delta > 0 && log.earnedToday(target) + delta > DAILY_CAP)\n      delta = Math.max(0, DAILY_CAP - log.earnedToday(target));\n    long entryId = log.append(target.id(), ev, sourcePostId, delta);\n    target.addReputation(delta, entryId);\n    target.clampReputationFloor(1);            // reputation never drops below 1\n  }\n\n  /** Retraction/deletion reverses the ORIGINAL entry — never re-derives a guessed delta. */\n  void reverse(long entryId) {\n    RepEntry e = log.get(entryId);\n    if (e.reversed()) return;                  // idempotent\n    log.markReversed(entryId);\n    User u = users.get(e.userId());\n    u.addReputation(-e.delta(), log.append(e.userId(), e.event(), e.sourcePostId(), -e.delta()));\n    u.clampReputationFloor(1);\n  }\n}\n\nclass VotingService {\n  private final VoteRepository votes; private final ReputationEngine rep; private final EventBus bus;\n\n  void vote(User voter, Post post, VoteType type) {\n    permissions.require(voter, type == VoteType.DOWN ? Privilege.DOWNVOTE : Privilege.UPVOTE);\n    if (post.author.equals(voter)) throw new AppError(\"cannot vote on your own post\");\n    Optional<Vote> existing = votes.find(voter.id(), post.id());   // unique (voter, post) index\n    if (existing.isPresent()) {\n      if (existing.get().type() == type) { retract(existing.get(), post); return; }\n      rep.reverse(existing.get().repEntryId());                     // undo old effect exactly\n      votes.delete(existing.get());\n    }\n    votes.insert(new Vote(voter.id(), post.id(), type));            // constraint = the guarantee\n    post.score += (type == VoteType.UP ? 1 : -1);\n    if (type == VoteType.UP)\n      rep.apply(post instanceof Question ? RepEvent.UPVOTE_QUESTION : RepEvent.UPVOTE_ANSWER,\n                post.author, post.id());\n    else {\n      rep.apply(RepEvent.DOWNVOTE_RECEIVED, post.author, post.id());\n      rep.apply(RepEvent.DOWNVOTE_CAST, voter, post.id());          // the voter pays too\n    }\n    bus.publish(new PostScored(post.id(), post.score));             // badges, search, feeds\n  }\n}\n\nenum Privilege {\n  COMMENT(50), UPVOTE(15), DOWNVOTE(125), EDIT_OTHERS(2000), CLOSE_VOTE(3000), MODERATE(10000);\n  final int threshold;\n  Privilege(int t) { threshold = t; }\n  boolean grantedTo(User u) { return u.isModerator() || u.reputation() >= threshold; }\n}"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: reputation as a rules engine, not arithmetic at call sites",
        "Watch what happens without the engine. The vote handler adds 10, the accept handler adds 15, the flag handler subtracts 100, and each of them also has to remember the caps. Then a vote is retracted, and the retraction path must reconstruct which delta was originally applied — but the rule may have CHANGED since, so re-deriving it from today's table silently corrupts the total. Then the post is deleted, and three separate handlers must each undo their own contribution. Then a user asks why they have 4,312 and nobody can answer. The engine fixes all four problems with one structural move: rules are DATA (a table of event type to delta, with caps and floors), effects are recorded in an APPEND-ONLY LEDGER of ReputationEvents, and the user's displayed reputation is a cached PROJECTION of that ledger. Four consequences worth stating explicitly. (1) EXACT REVERSAL: retraction reverses the stored entry by id, applying the negation of the delta that was ACTUALLY APPLIED — including a delta that was reduced by a daily cap — so a rule change never rewrites history, and reversal is idempotent because entries carry a reversed flag. (2) EXPLAINABILITY: the ledger IS the reputation history page; support and users read the same rows the system computed from. (3) RECONCILIATION: the cached total can be recomputed from the ledger and compared — a runnable audit for the single number users care most about. (4) RULE CHANGES ARE CONFIG: adjusting the accepted-answer bonus is a table edit. Now the details that separate a considered answer from a sketch. CAPS: daily reputation from upvotes is capped, but downvotes and accepted-answer bonuses are exempt — so caps are per-event-class, not global, and the cap must be applied AT WRITE TIME and stored, not applied at display. FLOOR: reputation never drops below 1, which means a −100 spam penalty against a user at 40 is partially absorbed, and if you reverse it later you must not hand back reputation that was never taken — another reason to store the applied delta. SELF-VOTING and vote fraud: the one-vote-per-user-per-post rule is enforced by a UNIQUE INDEX on (voterId, postId) so concurrent double-votes fail at the storage layer rather than passing a racy 'have they voted' check; serial voting rings are detected out of band and reversed through the same ledger. CASCADES: deleting a post reverses every reputation event sourced from it — one query on sourcePostId, which is exactly why that column exists."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the Post hierarchy, tags, and search",
        "The inheritance question is a judgement test, not a syntax test. Question and Answer share a genuinely large behavioural surface — they are votable, commentable, editable with revisions, flaggable, closable, deletable, and owned — so a Post base class is real reuse: the vote path, the moderation state machine, and the revision mechanism are written once and cannot drift between the two. What belongs in the subclasses is what is genuinely different: a Question has a title, tags, answers, an accepted answer, and a bounty; an Answer has a parent question id and an accepted flag. The interesting decision is COMMENT. It is tempting to make it a Post — it has an author and a body — but the base's behaviours are wrong for it: comments cannot be answered, cannot be downvoted, do not earn reputation, and have a hard length limit. Adding it forces the base class to carry exceptions, and exceptions in a base class are how hierarchies rot. The principle to state out loud is that inheritance should express shared BEHAVIOUR, not shared FIELDS; sharing an author and a timestamp is not a reason to inherit. Similarly, a Question is not a subclass of Answer and neither is a subclass of a generic 'Content' god-class — one level of meaningful abstraction is enough. TAGS are the second structural choice, and modelling them as plain strings on the question is the common mistake: real tags need descriptions, synonyms (js -> javascript), moderation, usage counts, and their own pages, so Tag is an entity and the relationship is many-to-many. That relationship is also what drives browsing, so it needs an index in both directions — question to tags for rendering, tag to questions for the tag page — which is a join table with indexes on both columns, or an inverted posting list. SEARCH follows from the same shape. Free-text search over titles and bodies is an INVERTED INDEX: tokenize, normalize (lowercase, stem), and map each term to a posting list of post ids; a query intersects postings and ranks. Ranking is where domain knowledge shows: score, accepted-answer presence, recency, view count, and term match quality are combined, and the pragmatic answer in an interview is that this belongs behind a SearchIndex interface backed by Elasticsearch rather than hand-rolled — but you should be able to describe the inverted index and why tag filtering is a cheap intersection on a pre-built posting list rather than a scan. Finally, the DENORMALIZED aggregates — score, answer count, view count — exist because the read path is overwhelming; they must be updated in the same transaction as the underlying record and be recomputable from it, which is another audit worth having."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: privileges as policy objects and the moderation lifecycle",
        "Privilege gating is where a clean domain model usually springs a leak. The leak looks like if (user.getReputation() >= 125) inside the downvote controller, another comparison inside the comment controller, a third inside the edit path — and then the product decides downvoting needs 200 reputation, and you are grepping for integers. Worse, moderators must bypass all of them, so every site grows an || user.isModerator() that someone forgets in one place, which is a security bug rather than a style complaint. The fix is small and worth presenting as a first-class part of the design: a PRIVILEGE is a named policy — a threshold plus a grantedTo(user) predicate — and all enforcement goes through one PrivilegeService with a require(user, privilege) that throws a typed authorization error. Now thresholds are configuration, the moderator override exists once, and the set of privileges is enumerable, which incidentally gives you the 'privileges' page users see for free. It also generalizes past thresholds without changing call sites: some privileges are not reputation at all — you may only accept an answer if you are the question's author, you may not vote on your own post, an account under a certain age is rate-limited, a suspended user has everything revoked — so a Privilege becomes a predicate over (user, target) and the call site still reads permissions.require(...). That is the Specification idea reused, and cross-referencing it is fine. THE MODERATION LIFECYCLE is the second half. A Post's state — ACTIVE, CLOSED, DELETED, LOCKED — is a small state machine with real rules: a closed question accepts no new answers but still accepts votes on existing ones; a deleted post is invisible to normal users but recoverable, and its deletion must REVERSE the reputation events it sourced; a locked post accepts no edits or votes at all. Encoding these as guarded transitions in one place beats scattering visibility checks through every read path. FLAGS and CLOSE VOTES are community mechanisms that reach a threshold and then trigger a transition — model a flag as a record, count distinct flaggers, and fire the transition when the count crosses; the vote-to-close is the same shape with a privilege gate. Two production details that earn credit: moderation actions must be AUDITED (who closed this, when, why) because they are contested, and every state change should publish an event so search indexing and caches follow without the moderation service knowing they exist."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "EVENT LEDGER vs COUNTER-ONLY reputation: a counter is one integer and one UPDATE, and it cannot answer 'why', cannot reverse accurately after a rule change, and cannot be audited; the ledger costs a row per event and storage growth but buys explainability, exact reversal, and recomputation — every real system keeps the ledger and caches the total, and the cache must be reconcilable against the log. SYNCHRONOUS vs ASYNCHRONOUS reputation application: applying inline keeps the number correct the instant the user refreshes but couples the vote request to more work; applying through an event bus scales and decouples badges and indexing at the cost of a visible lag, which users notice and complain about — most designs apply reputation synchronously and everything else (badges, search, feeds) asynchronously. DENORMALIZED SCORE vs COUNT-ON-READ: with the read-to-write ratio here, counting votes per render is indefensible; the trade is the usual one — the aggregate can drift, so it must be updated transactionally with the vote and periodically reconciled. COMMENT AS POST vs SEPARATE TYPE: unifying gives one moderation and edit path for free, at the cost of a base class full of 'not applicable for comments' behaviours; separating keeps the hierarchy honest and duplicates a little edit logic — most designs separate, and the reasoning matters more than the choice. INVERTED INDEX IN-HOUSE vs A SEARCH ENGINE: describing the index shows you understand the mechanism; using Elasticsearch behind an interface is the right engineering answer, and pretending otherwise in an interview reads as naive. Extensions to expect: BOUNTIES (reputation staked and escrowed on creation so it cannot be double-spent, then awarded or refunded — a small state machine that reuses the ledger); BADGES as rules evaluated against the event log, which is why the log's existence keeps paying off; DUPLICATE DETECTION and 'related questions' as similarity over the same index; REVISION HISTORY with rollback, already modelled; and REPUTATION RECALCULATION as a background job that replays the ledger — the runnable audit for the number the whole product is built on."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'A user retracts a downvote — what happens to both reputations?' — the engine reverses the STORED entries by id, restoring the author's −2 and the voter's −1 exactly as applied, including any cap adjustment; the current rule table is never used to re-derive an old delta. 'Product changes upvote-answer from +10 to +12 — do old votes change?' — no; historical entries are immutable, and only new events use the new table, which is the reason to store deltas rather than event types alone. 'A question is deleted — what about the reputation it generated?' — one query on sourcePostId reverses every entry sourced from it, idempotently. 'How do you stop double voting?' — a unique index on (voterId, postId); the application check is an optimization, the constraint is the guarantee, and a concurrent duplicate fails at insert. 'Reputation went negative from a spam penalty?' — clamp at a floor of 1 and store the delta actually applied, so a later reversal does not hand back reputation that was never removed. 'Where do you check that a user may downvote?' — permissions.require(user, Privilege.DOWNVOTE) — one service, thresholds as config, moderator override in exactly one place. 'Should Comment extend Post?' — no: it cannot be answered, downvoted, or earn reputation, so inheriting drags exceptions into the base; inherit behaviour, not fields. 'How does tag search work?' — tags are entities with a many-to-many join indexed both ways; free-text is an inverted index intersected with the tag posting list, ranked by score, acceptance, recency and match quality, and in practice delegated to a search engine behind an interface. 'How do you know the displayed reputation is right?' — recompute it from the ledger and diff against the cached total; that reconciliation job is the audit. 'How does this scale?' — reads dominate, so denormalized aggregates plus caching carry the question page; the ledger append is the write hot spot and shards by user; search and badges move behind the event bus, and the class model is unchanged — which is the point."),
    ],
    "Stack Overflow is the standard 'design a content platform with rules' LLD prompt, and interviewers use the reputation system as the probe. The weak answer sprinkles +10 and −2 through vote and accept handlers; the strong answer makes reputation an event-driven rules engine with the deltas as data, an append-only ledger, exact reversal by entry id, per-event caps and a floor — and then answers the retraction and deletion follow-ups without hesitating. The second probe is inheritance judgement: whether Question and Answer share a Post base for real behavioural reuse, and whether you can defend keeping Comment out of it. The third is privilege gating, where scattered reputation comparisons and a forgotten moderator override are a security bug; policy objects checked through one service fix both. Tags-as-entities and an inverted index for search round it out.",
    [
      G("scattered_reputation_math", "Adding and subtracting reputation inline in the vote, accept, and moderation handlers, with no ledger — so retractions re-derive deltas from the current rules and deletions cannot cascade.", "Emit domain events and let one ReputationEngine apply a data table of (event, role) -> delta, appending an immutable entry per effect; reverse by entry id so the exact applied delta (cap-adjusted and floor-clamped) is undone, and cascade deletions via sourcePostId.", "user.setReputation(user.getReputation() + 10) inside VotingService; retraction subtracting a hardcoded constant; no reputation history page; totals that cannot be recomputed from anything."),
      G("comment_in_post_hierarchy", "Forcing Comment (and sometimes Tag) into the Post hierarchy because it has an author and a body, leaving the base class carrying answers, downvotes, and reputation that do not apply.", "Inherit shared BEHAVIOUR, not shared fields: keep Question and Answer under Post for votes, revisions, comments and moderation, and give Comment its own small type; make Tag an entity with synonyms and usage counts rather than a string.", "Post subclasses whose methods throw UnsupportedOperationException; isComment() checks inside base-class methods; tags stored as a comma-separated string field; downvote paths guarded by 'if not a comment'."),
      G("inline_privilege_checks", "Comparing reputation to magic numbers at each call site (and enforcing one-vote-per-post with a read-then-insert check), with the moderator override repeated — or missing — per path.", "Route every gate through one PrivilegeService with named threshold policies so limits are configuration and the moderator override exists once; enforce one vote per user per post with a unique (voterId, postId) index rather than an application-level check.", "if (user.getReputation() > 125) in a controller; the same threshold literal in several files; a path missing || isModerator(); duplicate votes appearing under concurrency."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.stackoverflow, "Stack Overflow class diagram"),

  // ─────────────────── MOVIE BOOKING SYSTEM ─────────────────────────────────
  TT("lld_m8", "lld_m8_t4", 4, "Design a Movie Booking System", "design-movie-booking",
    ["case-study", "concurrency", "state-machine", "reliability"],
    "Friday, 6:59 PM. Two hundred people are staring at the same seat map for the 7:15 show, and two of them tap seats E7 and E8 within the same millisecond. Both then spend four minutes at a payment gateway. Exactly one may end up holding those seats — and if the other abandons the flow, the seats must come back without a human touching anything. Design the Movie Booking System.",
    "One invariant defines this design: NO SEAT IS EVER CONFIRMED TWICE. Everything else is arranged to serve it. The mechanism is a two-phase reservation — an atomic conditional transition of the WHOLE requested seat set from AVAILABLE to HELD, followed by payment, followed by HELD to BOOKED — where the hold carries a TTL so an abandoned checkout self-heals. The failure everyone writes first is check-then-act: read the seat map, see green, then write. That races. The correct primitive is a conditional write whose matched-count IS the answer, and the honest ending is that the in-process lock you draw on the whiteboard becomes a DATABASE UNIQUE CONSTRAINT the moment there are two app servers.",
    [
      C("requirements", "Requirements",
        "Functional: users browse MOVIES by city, pick a cinema and a SHOW (a movie playing on a specific screen at a specific time), see a live SEAT MAP with each seat's availability and price tier, select one or more seats, and pay. Successful payment produces a BOOKING with a reference, a ticket, and a confirmation notification. Users can cancel within a policy window for a refund, and cinema staff can block seats for maintenance or hold them for house use. Pricing varies by seat class (recliner, premium, regular), by show time (matinee vs prime), by day, and by offers or coupons. Admin surfaces manage cinemas, screens, seat layouts, and show scheduling — including the constraint that two shows on one screen cannot overlap. Non-functional requirements are the design: THE CORE INVARIANT is that a given seat for a given show is confirmed to at most one booking, ever, and this must hold under a Friday-evening thundering herd on a popular show; the flow includes a HUMAN-SCALE PAUSE — payment takes seconds to minutes and can fail or be abandoned — so seats must be held during it and released automatically afterwards, which means the design cannot be a single transaction; reads dominate writes by orders of magnitude (everyone views the seat map, few book), so the map must be cheap to serve and can tolerate being a moment stale as long as the WRITE path is authoritative; and no seat may be leaked — a crashed server mid-hold must not remove a seat from sale forever. Clarify scope: the booking domain and its concurrency protocol, payment behind a gateway interface, storage behind repositories."),
      C("entities", "Core entities & responsibilities",
        "CITY, CINEMA and SCREEN are the venue hierarchy; a Screen owns a fixed SEAT layout — Seat is physical and immutable (row, number, class), which is the distinction people blur. SHOW is the sellable unit: a movie, a screen, a start time, and a duration, plus its pricing configuration. SHOWSEAT is the crossing of Show and Seat and is where every interesting thing happens: (showId, seatId) with a STATE — AVAILABLE, HELD, BOOKED, BLOCKED — plus holdId, holdExpiresAt, and bookingId. Modelling ShowSeat separately from Seat is the design's first correct move: seats are physical and shared across shows, availability is per show, and conflating them means one show's booking marks the chair occupied for every other screening that night. SEATHOLD is the temporary claim: holdId, showId, the seat id set, userId, createdAt, expiresAt — and its expiry is what makes abandonment self-healing. BOOKING is the durable record with its own state machine — PENDING (hold taken, awaiting payment), CONFIRMED, EXPIRED, CANCELLED, FAILED — plus the seat list, the total, and a payment reference. PAYMENT wraps the gateway interaction with an idempotency key equal to the booking id, so a retried or replayed webhook cannot charge twice. PRICINGSTRATEGY computes the price for a (show, seat) pair, composed with offers — the varying-algorithm slot in an otherwise structural design. BOOKINGSERVICE is the facade sequencing hold, price, pay, confirm, and compensate. EXPIRYREAPER releases stale holds, and NOTIFICATIONSERVICE (via an event) sends the ticket. Deliberately absent: any global lock, and any notion of seat state living on Seat."),
      C("design", "Class design & patterns",
        "The pipeline is deliberately three phases and it is worth defending the shape before the code. PHASE 1, HOLD: validate the request, then attempt an ATOMIC CONDITIONAL TRANSITION of every requested ShowSeat from AVAILABLE to HELD under one holdId — all or nothing, because a partial hold on three of four seats is a worse outcome than a clean failure and would leave the user with a split party and the system with orphaned claims. PHASE 2, PAY: the user is off at a gateway for minutes; the hold's TTL is the only thing protecting the seats, and no lock of any kind is held across this — holding a database lock across a payment call is the classic disaster, because a slow gateway then pins connections and stalls the whole service. PHASE 3, CONFIRM: on payment success, transition HELD to BOOKED but only if the hold is still valid; on failure or timeout, release. Patterns, each earning its place: STATE (as a guarded transition method rather than a class hierarchy — four states do not justify four classes, and saying so is signal) for ShowSeat and Booking, so 'confirm an expired hold' is rejected in one place; STRATEGY for pricing, since seat class, time slot, day, and offers compose into a genuinely varying computation; FACADE for BookingService; OBSERVER for post-confirmation side effects (ticket email, analytics, loyalty) so payment code does not import a mailer; and REPOSITORY interfaces so the atomic operation can be expressed as an in-memory CAS in the interview and a conditional UPDATE in production without the service changing. Two structural rules to state explicitly: the WRITE PATH IS AUTHORITATIVE and the read path (the seat map) may be cached and slightly stale — users will occasionally tap a seat someone just took, and the correct product behaviour is a clean failure at hold time, not a lock on browsing. And ORDERING: when acquiring multiple seats in-process, always acquire in a canonical order (sorted seat id) so two overlapping multi-seat requests cannot deadlock each other."),
      K("code", "Core classes (Java)",
        "enum SeatState { AVAILABLE, HELD, BOOKED, BLOCKED }\n\nclass ShowSeat {\n  final String showId, seatId;\n  private SeatState state = SeatState.AVAILABLE;\n  private String holdId, bookingId;\n  private Instant holdExpiresAt;\n\n  /** Lazy expiry: a stale hold is treated as AVAILABLE the moment anyone looks. */\n  synchronized boolean isFree(Instant now) {\n    if (state == SeatState.HELD && now.isAfter(holdExpiresAt)) {\n      state = SeatState.AVAILABLE; holdId = null; holdExpiresAt = null;\n    }\n    return state == SeatState.AVAILABLE;\n  }\n  synchronized boolean tryHold(String holdId, Instant now, Duration ttl) {\n    if (!isFree(now)) return false;                 // check AND act, under one monitor\n    state = SeatState.HELD; this.holdId = holdId; this.holdExpiresAt = now.plus(ttl);\n    return true;\n  }\n  synchronized boolean confirm(String holdId, String bookingId, Instant now) {\n    if (state != SeatState.HELD || !this.holdId.equals(holdId) || now.isAfter(holdExpiresAt))\n      return false;                                 // hold expired or belongs to someone else\n    state = SeatState.BOOKED; this.bookingId = bookingId; this.holdExpiresAt = null;\n    return true;\n  }\n  synchronized void release(String holdId) {\n    if (state == SeatState.HELD && this.holdId.equals(holdId)) {\n      state = SeatState.AVAILABLE; this.holdId = null; this.holdExpiresAt = null;\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nclass BookingService {\n  private static final Duration HOLD_TTL = Duration.ofMinutes(8);\n  private final ShowSeatRepository seats; private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n  private final PricingStrategy pricing; private final EventBus bus;\n\n  /** ALL-OR-NOTHING hold. Canonical order prevents two multi-seat requests deadlocking. */\n  Optional<SeatHold> hold(String showId, List<String> seatIds, String userId) {\n    List<String> ordered = seatIds.stream().sorted().toList();\n    String holdId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n    Instant now = Instant.now();\n    List<ShowSeat> taken = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (String sid : ordered) {\n      ShowSeat ss = seats.get(showId, sid);\n      if (ss.tryHold(holdId, now, HOLD_TTL)) { taken.add(ss); continue; }\n      taken.forEach(t -> t.release(holdId));        // compensate: give back what we grabbed\n      return Optional.empty();\n    }\n    return Optional.of(new SeatHold(holdId, showId, ordered, userId, now.plus(HOLD_TTL)));\n  }\n\n  Booking checkout(SeatHold hold, PaymentDetails pd) {\n    long amount = hold.seatIds().stream()\n        .mapToLong(s -> pricing.priceFor(hold.showId(), s)).sum();\n    Booking b = Booking.pending(hold, amount);      // persisted BEFORE the gateway call\n\n    // NO lock is held across this call: it takes seconds and can fail. The TTL guards the seats.\n    PaymentResult r = gateway.charge(pd, amount, b.id());   // idempotency key = booking id\n\n    if (!r.success()) { releaseAll(hold); b.transitionTo(BookingState.FAILED); return b; }\n\n    Instant now = Instant.now();\n    List<ShowSeat> confirmed = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (String sid : hold.seatIds()) {\n      ShowSeat ss = seats.get(hold.showId(), sid);\n      if (ss.confirm(hold.holdId(), b.id(), now)) { confirmed.add(ss); continue; }\n      // Hold expired while paying: the money is real, the seats are not. Compensate honestly.\n      confirmed.forEach(c -> c.release(hold.holdId()));\n      gateway.refund(r.transactionId(), amount, b.id());\n      b.transitionTo(BookingState.EXPIRED);\n      return b;\n    }\n    b.transitionTo(BookingState.CONFIRMED);\n    bus.publish(new BookingConfirmed(b.id()));      // ticket, email, analytics — off this path\n    return b;\n  }\n\n  private void releaseAll(SeatHold h) {\n    h.seatIds().forEach(s -> seats.get(h.showId(), s).release(h.holdId()));\n  }\n}\n\n/*\n * Replicated deployment: the synchronized blocks above protect nothing across JVMs.\n * The SAME protocol expressed as one conditional write, where matched-count IS the answer:\n *\n *   UPDATE show_seat SET state='HELD', hold_id=?, hold_expires_at=?\n *    WHERE show_id=? AND seat_id IN (?,?,?)\n *      AND (state='AVAILABLE' OR (state='HELD' AND hold_expires_at < now()))\n *   -- succeeded iff rowsAffected == seatIds.size(); otherwise roll back the transaction.\n *\n * Plus the belt-and-braces guarantee: UNIQUE INDEX (show_id, seat_id) on confirmed bookings,\n * so even a logic bug cannot produce two confirmed bookings for one seat.\n */"),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the seat-hold mechanism and TTL expiry",
        "Why does a hold exist at all? Because the booking flow contains a human and a third party: the user selects seats, then spends anywhere from ten seconds to several minutes at a payment gateway, possibly on 3-D Secure, possibly on a flaky phone connection. The two naive alternatives both fail. Reserving only at payment SUCCESS means two users can both pay for E7 and one gets a refund and a ruined evening — you have converted a concurrency bug into a customer-service incident. Reserving PERMANENTLY at selection means every abandoned cart removes seats from sale forever, and a popular show sells out to nobody. The hold is the middle: a TEMPORARY, EXPIRING claim strong enough to exclude other buyers and weak enough to heal itself. Design details that matter. TTL LENGTH is a product trade — too short and slow payers lose their seats mid-transaction (support calls, chargebacks); too long and abandoned carts starve inventory during peak; five to ten minutes is typical, shown to the user as a visible countdown, which is a UX feature that exists purely to make the concurrency model comprehensible. EXPIRY MUST BE BOTH LAZY AND SWEPT. Lazy: any read or write of a ShowSeat treats a HELD state whose holdExpiresAt has passed as AVAILABLE — that is the isFree() check above, and it is what guarantees correctness, because it means an expired hold can never block a sale even if no background job ever runs. Swept: a reaper job periodically materializes those expirations so the cached seat map and analytics reflect reality and so holds do not accumulate. Relying ONLY on the reaper is the bug — if the job dies, seats silently vanish from sale; relying only on lazy expiry leaves stale maps. Both, with lazy as the authority. THE HOLD MUST BE ALL-OR-NOTHING across the requested set: a family wants four seats together, and three-of-four is a failure, not a partial success — so a failed seat rolls back the ones already taken, which is a compensating action rather than a transaction rollback once the store is remote. IDENTITY: the holdId is what makes release and confirm safe — release(holdId) only releases if the caller still owns the hold, so a late release from a request whose hold already expired and was re-held by someone else cannot steal the new owner's seats. That guard is the sort of detail interviewers reward, because it is the exact bug that appears in production two weeks after launch. Finally, allow a user to RE-ENTER their own hold (same user, same seats, extend or reuse) so a refresh does not lose the claim."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: the concurrency invariant — atomic conditional reservation, and locks that become constraints",
        "State the invariant as a sentence you would put in a test name: for any (show, seat), at most one CONFIRMED booking exists, ever. Now walk the failure. The naive implementation reads the seat map, sees E7 AVAILABLE, and writes HELD. Two requests interleave between the read and the write; both see AVAILABLE; both write; the last writer's holdId wins and the first user proceeds to pay for a seat they do not own — TIME OF CHECK TO TIME OF USE, the same bug as the ATM's cassette and the course registrar's last seat, and recognizing that these are one bug wearing three costumes is what an interviewer is measuring. The repair is to make the check and the write ONE OPERATION whose result IS the decision. In-process that is the synchronized tryHold above — the state test and the mutation happen under the seat's own monitor, so no interleaving exists; the lock is per SEAT (or per show), never global, because a global lock serializes every unrelated cinema in the country through one mutex. Across a replicated deployment those monitors protect nothing, and the honest continuation — the one that separates candidates — is that the SAME protocol re-expresses as a CONDITIONAL WRITE: a single UPDATE that sets HELD for the requested seats WHERE the state is AVAILABLE or the existing hold has expired, succeeding if and only if rowsAffected equals the number of seats requested; anything less rolls the transaction back and the user is told the seats went. That predicate in the WHERE clause is the lazy expiry and the atomicity in one statement. The equivalent primitives, worth naming so the interviewer knows you have options: OPTIMISTIC LOCKING with a version column (UPDATE ... WHERE version = ?), which retries on conflict and suits low contention; SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the seat rows inside a short transaction, which is pessimistic and fine as long as it is never held across the payment call; and a Redis SET NX with a TTL per seat key, which is fast and expiring by construction but is a second source of truth that must reconcile with the database. Whatever the mechanism, add the BELT AND BRACES: a UNIQUE CONSTRAINT on (showId, seatId) over confirmed bookings, so that even a logic bug in the application cannot produce the double-booking — the database enforces the invariant the design promised. Two more rules complete it: acquire multiple seats in a canonical order so overlapping requests cannot deadlock, and never hold any lock across an external call. If you say only one thing about this system, say that the invariant is enforced by a constraint, not by a check."),
      C("deep_dive", "Deep dive: payment, expiry races, and the compensation you cannot avoid",
        "The nastiest case in this design is not two users racing for a seat — it is one user whose hold expires WHILE the payment succeeds. The money is real and the seats are gone. There is no way to make the charge and the seat transition atomic (different systems, one of them external), so the design's job is to make every outcome DETECTABLE and COMPENSABLE, which is the same reframing the ATM needed. The protocol: persist the Booking as PENDING with its seat list BEFORE calling the gateway, so a crash mid-payment leaves a record to reconcile; use the booking id as the gateway IDEMPOTENCY KEY, so a retry or a duplicated webhook cannot double-charge; on return, attempt the confirm transition guarded by the holdId AND the expiry; if any seat fails to confirm, release the ones that did, REFUND, and put the booking in EXPIRED with a clear message. Practical mitigations worth volunteering: make the hold TTL comfortably longer than the gateway's own timeout so the window is rare; grant a short GRACE PERIOD where a hold that expired seconds ago but has not been re-taken can still confirm (check that nobody else now holds the seat — the holdId guard makes this safe); and if seats were re-sold, refund immediately and automatically rather than leaving it to support. WEBHOOKS are the second half: gateways confirm asynchronously and deliver at-least-once and out of order, so the webhook handler must be idempotent (look up by booking id, ignore anything not in PENDING), and there must be a RECONCILIATION job that finds PENDING bookings older than the TTL and queries the gateway for their true status — because a webhook that never arrives is not a rare event, it is a Tuesday. CANCELLATION runs the transitions backwards: the booking moves to CANCELLED, the seats go back to AVAILABLE, and the refund goes through policy (full, partial, or none by cutoff), with the seat release and refund each idempotent so a double-clicked cancel is harmless. The READ PATH deserves a closing note because it is where the system's load actually lives: the seat map is served from a cache invalidated by seat-state events, is allowed to be a second stale, and the resulting occasional 'that seat just went' at hold time is CORRECT behaviour, not a defect — the authority is the conditional write, and no amount of read-path locking would improve on it."),
      C("tradeoffs", "Trade-offs & extension points",
        "PESSIMISTIC (SELECT FOR UPDATE) vs OPTIMISTIC (version column) vs CONDITIONAL UPDATE: pessimistic is simple and predictable but holds row locks and must never span a payment; optimistic is lock-free and great under low contention but degenerates into retry storms exactly on the popular Friday show; the single conditional UPDATE with a matched-count check is usually the best of the three here because it is one round trip and self-expiring — pick by contention shape and say why. HOLD IN THE DATABASE vs IN REDIS: the database keeps one source of truth and gets the unique constraint for free; Redis with SET NX and a native TTL is faster and expires without a reaper, but introduces a second store that can disagree with the ledger after a failover — acceptable when the DB still holds the confirmed-booking constraint as the final authority. LAZY EXPIRY vs REAPER: lazy is the correctness mechanism, the reaper is the housekeeping; running only the reaper risks silently unsellable seats if the job dies. SHORT vs LONG TTL: inventory starvation versus lost sales from timeouts — a countdown in the UI converts the trade into an explicit user contract. SEAT-LEVEL vs SHOW-LEVEL locking: per-seat maximizes concurrency and needs canonical ordering to avoid deadlock on multi-seat requests; a per-show lock is trivially correct and serializes an entire sold-out blockbuster through one mutex, which is exactly the wrong place to serialize. FAN-OUT READS: caching the seat map trades staleness for the ability to serve two hundred concurrent viewers per show. Extensions to expect: SEAT ADJACENCY and best-available auto-selection (a scan over the row layout with a no-single-gap rule, a genuinely nice algorithmic follow-up); DYNAMIC PRICING and offers, which compose as pricing strategies; GROUP BOOKINGS and partial cancellation, which stress the all-or-nothing assumption; a VIRTUAL WAITING ROOM in front of the service for tickets that go on sale at a fixed instant, which is where this LLD hands off to system design; and OVERSELLING as a deliberate business policy (airlines do it, cinemas do not) — a good closing remark, because it shows you know the invariant is a product decision that this design happens to enforce absolutely."),
      C("deep_dive", "Common follow-ups & how it evolves",
        "Expect: 'Two users tap the same seat in the same millisecond.' — both attempt the atomic conditional transition; exactly one matches the AVAILABLE-or-expired predicate and gets the hold, the other gets a clean failure and a refreshed map. There is no window between check and write because there is no separate check. 'The user abandons at the payment page.' — nothing needs to happen: the hold has a TTL, and the next reader treats the expired hold as AVAILABLE; the reaper tidies up asynchronously so the cached map stays honest. 'Payment succeeded but the hold expired.' — persist the booking as PENDING before charging, use the booking id as the gateway idempotency key, and on a failed confirm release whatever did confirm and refund automatically, marking the booking EXPIRED; optionally allow a short grace confirm if nobody else has taken the seat. 'Three of four seats are available.' — the hold is all-or-nothing; the three taken are released and the request fails, because a split party is not a successful booking. 'Two multi-seat requests overlap.' — acquire seats in canonical (sorted) order so a cycle is impossible; with a single conditional UPDATE over the whole set the question disappears entirely. 'Now run two app servers.' — the synchronized blocks stop meaning anything; the same protocol becomes a conditional UPDATE whose rowsAffected must equal the seat count, and a unique constraint on (showId, seatId) for confirmed bookings backs it up so a logic bug still cannot double-book. 'The webhook arrives twice, or never.' — handle only bookings in PENDING (idempotent by booking id), and run a reconciliation job that queries the gateway for stale PENDING bookings. 'Is the seat map allowed to be stale?' — yes; reads are cached and eventually consistent, and an occasional failed hold is the correct, cheap outcome — the write path is the authority. 'What would you add for a blockbuster on-sale at noon?' — a virtual waiting room and queueing ahead of the service; the seat protocol itself does not change, which is the sign the invariant was placed correctly."),
    ],
    "Movie booking is the most common concurrency-flavoured LLD prompt after parking lot, and every serious interviewer drives at the same point: two users, one seat, what exactly prevents the double booking. Candidates who answer 'check if the seat is free, then mark it' have handed over the bug; the expected answer names check-then-act as TOCTOU and replaces it with an atomic conditional transition — a per-seat guarded transition in-process, a conditional UPDATE with a matched-count check across replicas, and a unique constraint as the final backstop. The second axis is the hold: interviewers ask what happens when the user walks away, and the strong answer volunteers a TTL, lazy expiry as the correctness mechanism with a reaper for housekeeping, all-or-nothing multi-seat holds, and holdId-guarded release. The finish is the payment window — persisting PENDING before charging, idempotency keyed by booking id, and automatic refund when a hold expired mid-payment — because that is what separates people who have shipped checkout flows from people who have drawn them.",
    [
      G("check_then_book", "Reading the seat map, seeing the seat free, and then writing the reservation as a separate step — or holding the seat only after payment succeeds.", "Fuse check and act into one atomic conditional transition: a guarded per-seat transition in-process, a conditional UPDATE whose rowsAffected must equal the seat count across replicas, plus a unique constraint on (showId, seatId) for confirmed bookings as the final backstop.", "if (seat.isAvailable()) seat.book(); a global or per-cinema lock instead of per-seat; seats marked only in the payment callback; duplicate confirmed bookings found in production for one seat."),
      G("holds_without_ttl_or_ownership", "Holding seats with no expiry (or relying solely on a background reaper), releasing without checking who owns the hold, and allowing partial holds on a multi-seat request.", "Give every hold a TTL and treat an expired hold as AVAILABLE lazily on every read/write (the reaper is housekeeping, not correctness); guard release and confirm by holdId so a late request cannot steal a re-held seat; make multi-seat holds all-or-nothing with compensating releases.", "Seats permanently unavailable after abandoned checkouts; release() that clears state regardless of holdId; three of four requested seats held and the request still returning success; expiry implemented only as a cron job."),
      G("lock_across_payment", "Holding a database transaction or lock across the payment gateway call, and treating charge-then-confirm as if it were atomic — no PENDING record, no idempotency key, no refund path.", "Never hold a lock across an external call: persist the booking as PENDING before charging, key the gateway call by the booking id, confirm under a holdId-and-expiry guard afterwards, and release plus refund automatically when confirmation fails; reconcile stale PENDING bookings against the gateway.", "SELECT ... FOR UPDATE spanning gateway.charge(); connection-pool exhaustion when the gateway is slow; double charges on webhook retries; users charged with no seats and no automatic refund."),
    ],
    0.4, DIAG.booking, "Movie booking system class diagram"),
];

const xpFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 5 : l === "easy" ? 10 : l === "medium" ? 15 : 20);
const diffFor = (l) => (l === "warmup" ? 0.1 : l === "easy" ? 0.2 : l === "medium" ? 0.4 : 0.6);
function pm(moduleId, o) {
  return { trackKey: TRACK_KEY, moduleId, topicId: o.topicId, exerciseId: o.exerciseId,
    position: o.position, level: o.level, type: "pattern_match", title: o.title, scenario: o.scenario,
    instructions: "Pick the best answer.", starterCode: "", expectedSolution: o.correct,
    blanks: [{ options: o.options }], testCases: [{ type: "pattern_match", correct: o.correct, explanation: o.explanation }],
    hints: [], xpReward: xpFor(o.level), difficulty: diffFor(o.level) };
}

